Because of the symmetry, we first let  $x,y,z\in\mathbb{N}$ . Then initially we get the result   $$ \left\{\begin{array}{l}
\frac{1}{x^2}\leq\frac{2}{3}\vspace{0.05in}
\frac{2}{y^2}\leq\frac{2}{3}\vspace{0.05in}
\frac{3}{z^2}\leq\frac{2}{3}\end{array}\right.\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\left\{\begin{array}{l}
x\geq 2\vspace{0.05in}
y\geq 2\vspace{0.05in}
z\geq 3\end{array}\right. $$  Case 1:  $x=2$ . ( $\Longrightarrow\frac{2}{y^2}+\frac{3}{z^2}=\frac{5}{12}$ .)
It can be seen that if  $y\in\{2,3,4\}$ , then the equation fails. If  $y\geq 5$ , then  $$ \frac{2}{y^2}+\frac{3}{z^2}\leq\frac{2}{25}+\frac{1}{3}=\frac{31}{75}<\frac{5}{12} $$  Hence we conclude that Case 1 fails.
Case 2:  $y=2$ . ( $\Longrightarrow\frac{1}{x^2}+\frac{3}{z^2}=\frac{1}{6}$ .)
In this case, we need  $z\geq 5$ , and by Case 1, we need  $x\geq 3$ . Now, it can be seen that if  $x\in\{3,4\}$ , then the equation fails. However, if  $x\geq 5$ , then  $$ \frac{1}{x^2}+\frac{3}{z^2}\leq\frac{1}{25}+\frac{3}{25}=\frac{4}{25}<\frac{1}{6} $$  Hence we conclude that Case 2 fails, and next we propose Case 3.
Case 3:  $x,y,z\geq3$ .
It can be seen that if  $x=y=z=3$ , then the equation holds. Moreover, by the preceding cases, we only have this solution, and finish this case.
Finally, by extending this solution to  $\mathbb{Z}$ , we conclude that there are eight solutions  $(x,y,z)=(\pm 3,\pm 3,\pm 3)$ .

