<details><summary>solution</summary>We first note that it holds for  $m,n=0$ .
Suppose that it holds for  $m,n$ . We now induct on  $n$  and show that it also holds for  $m,n+1$ . This can then be done in the same way for induction on  $m$ .

Then for  $m,n+1$ , the LHS turns out to be
\begin{align*}
x^{m+1} \sum_{j=0}^{n+1} \binom{m+j}{j} y^j + y^{n+2} \sum_{i=0}^m \binom{n+1+i}{i} x^i &= x^{m+1} \sum_{j=0}^{n} \binom{m+j}{j} y^j + {m+n+1 \choose n+1}x^{m+1}y^{n+1} + y^{n+2} \sum_{i=0}^m \binom{n+1+i}{i} x^i 
&= x^{m+1} \sum_{j=0}^{n} \binom{m+j}{j} y^j + {m+n+1 \choose n+1}x^{m+1}y^{n+1} + y^{n+1} \sum_{i=0}^m y\cdot\frac{n+1+i}{n+1}\binom{n+i}{i} x^i 
&= \left(1-y^{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^m{n+i\choose i}x^i\right) + {m+n+1 \choose n+1}x^{m+1}y^{n+1} + y^{n+1} \sum_{i=0}^m y\cdot\frac{n+1+i}{n+1}\binom{n+i}{i} x^i 
\end{align*}
So we want to prove   $$ y^{n+1}\left({m+n+1 \choose n+1}x^{m+1}-\sum_{i=0}^m{n+i\choose i}x^i + \frac{y}{n+1}\cdot\sum_{i=0}^m (n+1+i)\binom{n+i}{i} x^i\right)=0 \, , $$  that is 
\begin{align*}
{m+n+1 \choose n+1}x^{m+1}-\sum_{i=0}^m{n+i\choose i}x^i + \frac{y}{n+1}\cdot\sum_{i=0}^m (n+1+i)\binom{n+i}{i} x^i = 0 \Longleftrightarrow 
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=0}^m{n+i\choose i}x^i&={m+n+1 \choose n+1}x^{m+1} + \frac{y}{n+1}\cdot\sum_{i=0}^m (n+1+i)\binom{n+i}{i} x^i  \Longleftrightarrow
(n+1)\sum_{i=0}^m{n+i\choose i}x^i&=(m+n+1){m+n\choose n}x^{m+1}+y\sum_{i=0}^m (n+1+i)\binom{n+i}{i} x^i \Longleftrightarrow
(1-x) \sum_{i=0}^m (n+1+i)\binom{n+i}{i} x^i &= (n+1)\sum_{i=0}^m{n+i\choose i}x^i -(m+n+1){m+n\choose n}x^{m+1} \Longleftrightarrow 
\sum_{i=0}^m (n+1+i)\binom{n+i}{i} x^i &= (n+1)\sum_{i=0}^m{n+i\choose i}x^i+\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}(n+1+i){n+i\choose i}x^{i+1} \Longleftrightarrow 
\sum_{i=0}^m (n+1+i)\binom{n+i}{i} x^i &= (n+1)\sum_{i=0}^m{n+i\choose i}x^i+\sum_{i=1}^{m}(n+i){n+i-1\choose i-1}x^{i}
\end{align*}
Finally we have to prove
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=0}^m \left((n+1+i)\binom{n+i}{i}-(n+i){n+i-1\choose i-1}\right)x^i 
&=\sum_{i=0}^m \left((n+1+i)\binom{n+i}{i}-i{n+i\choose i}\right)x^i
&=\sum_{i=0}^m (n+1){n+i\choose i}x^i \, ,
\end{align*}
which is seen to be true.

This concludes the problem.  $\square$</details>

