We claim our answer is  $\boxed{\left(\frac{p-1}{2}, \frac{p+1}{2}\right)}$ . Notice this gives
\begin{align*}
(\sqrt x + \sqrt y)^2 &= x+y + \sqrt{4xy} 
&= p + \sqrt{p^2-1}.
\end{align*}

Notice the only way to get this expression closer to  $2p$  would be to get the value inside the square root up, as we can't have  $\sqrt{4xy}$  be an integer. Yet this would require  $4xy > p^2$ , which makes  $x+y < p$ , contradiction by AM-GM.  $\blacksquare$

