The condition  $a\equiv b\pmod{n}\implies ab\equiv 1\pmod{n}$  implies that  $a^2\equiv 1\pmod{n}$  for all  $a$  relatively prime to  $n$ . Let  $n=2^kp_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\ldots p_k^{e_k}$ . By CRT, we must have  $$ \begin{cases}
a^2\equiv 1\pmod{2^k}
a^2\equiv 1\pmod{p_1^{e_1}}
a^2\equiv 1\pmod{p_2^{e_2}}
\qquad\vdots
a^2\equiv 1\pmod{p_k^{e_k}}
\end{cases} $$  Now, it is well known that for any  $p_m^{e_m}$ , we can pick a primitive root  $g$  and set  $a=g$ . This implies  $\text{ord}_{p_m^{e_m}}(a)=\varphi(p_m^{e_m})>2$  unless there doesn't exist any  $p_m$  or  $p_1=3$  and  $e_1=1$  and we have no other primes.

*Case 1:*  $p_m$  does not exist.

Then,  $n=2^k$  for some positive integer  $k$ . This implies  $$ a^2\equiv 1\pmod{2^k} $$  However, consider  $a=3$ , then we have  $$ 3^2\equiv 9\equiv 1\pmod{2^k} $$  which is not true for  $k>3$ . Thus, we must have  $k\le 3$ . Testing each case, we see that all three work. So,  $n=2,4,8$  are all valid solutions.

*Case 2:*  $p_1=3$  and  $e_1=1$ .

Thus,  $n=3\cdot 2^k$  for some non-negative integer  $k$ . By CRT, we must have  $$ \begin{cases}
a^2\equiv 1\pmod{3}
a^2\equiv 1\pmod{2^k}
\end{cases} $$  Consider  $a=5$ . Then, we must have  $$ 5^2\equiv 25\equiv 1\pmod{2^k} $$  which is not true for all  $k>4$ . Thus, it suffices to test  $k=0,1,2,3,4$ . Testing each of them, we see that only  $k=0,1,2,3$  work. This gives us the solutions  $n=3,6,12,24$ . 

In conclusion, the only values for  $n$  that work are  $\boxed{n=2,3,4,6,8,12,24}$  and we are done.

