Let  $\theta_1 = \cos^{-1} \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + x_1^2}}$  and for all  $i \ge 2$ , let \[\theta_i = \cos^{-1} \frac{\sqrt{1 + x_1^2+ \cdots + x_{i - 1}^2}}{\sqrt{1 + x_1^2 + \cdots + x_{i - 1}^2 + x_{i}^2}}.\]
    We wish to show that \[\sum_{i = 1}^n \frac{x_i}{1 + x_1^2 + \cdots + x_i^2} = \sum_{i = 1}^n \cos\theta_1 \cos\theta_2 \cdots \cos\theta_i \sin \theta_i < \sqrt{n}.\]
    To prove that  $\sum_{i = 1}^n \cos \theta_1 \cdots \cos \theta_i \sin \theta_i < \sqrt{n}$  holds for any  $\theta_i$ , we will induct on  $n$ . The base case  $n = 1$  is clear because \[\cos \theta_1 \sin \theta_1 = \frac{1}{2} \sin(2\theta_1) < 1.\]
