Erect squares outside of  $\triangle ABC$  with  $BC,CA,AB$  as one of their sides each. Let the centers of these squares be  $A',B',C'$  respectively. Note that  $A,A_1,A'$  are collinear because of a homothety centered at  $A$ . Similarly,  $B_1$  lies on  $BB'$  and  $C_1$  lies on  $CC'$ . Therefore, it suffices to show that  $AA',BB',CC'$  concur.

We will use complex numbers. Assume without loss of generality that  $A,B,C$  are arranged in counterclockwise order, and let  $a,b,c$  be the complex numbers representing  $A,B,C$ , and furthermore let the circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$  be the unit circle. Then, the vertex opposite  $C$  of the square with  $BC$  as one of its sides is  $-i(c-b)+b$ , and so   $$ a'=\frac{-i(c-b)+b+c}{2}=\frac{b+c+ib-ic}{2}. $$   By symmetry,   $$ b'=\frac{c+a+ic-ia}{2}\text{ and } c'=\frac{a+b+ia-ib}{2}. $$  **Claim:**  $AA'\perp B'C'$ .
*Proof:* We will show that  $\frac{a-a'}{b'-c'}$  is purely imaginary. Scaling up by  $2$  to get rid of some fractions, this is equivalent to   $$ \frac{2a-b-c-ib+ic}{c-b+ib+ic-2ia}\in i\mathbb{R}. $$  Notice that   $$ \overline{\left(\frac{2a-b-c-ib+ic}{c-b+ib+ic-2ia}\right)}=\frac{\frac{2}{a}-\frac{1}{b}-\frac{1}{c}-\frac{1}{ib}+\frac{1}{ic}}{\frac{1}{c}-\frac{1}{b}+\frac{1}{ib}+\frac{1}{ic}-\frac{2}{ia}} $$    $$ =\frac{\frac{2}{a}-\frac{1}{b}-\frac{1}{c}+\frac{i}{b}-\frac{i}{c}}{\frac{1}{c}-\frac{1}{b}-\frac{i}{b}-\frac{i}{c}+\frac{2i}{a}}. $$  After multiplying out, it can be seen that   $$ (2a-b-c-ib+ic)\left(\frac{1}{c}-\frac{1}{b}-\frac{i}{b}-\frac{i}{c}+\frac{2i}{a}\right) $$   is equal to   $$ \frac{2a}{c}-\frac{2a}{b}-\frac{2ai}{b}-\frac{2ai}{c}-\frac{2bi}{a}-\frac{2ci}{a}+\frac{2b}{a}-\frac{2c}{a}-\frac{2b}{c}+\frac{2c}{b}+8i, $$  while   $$ (c-b+ib+ic-2ia)\left(\frac{2}{a}-\frac{1}{b}-\frac{1}{c}+\frac{i}{b}-\frac{i}{c}\right) $$   is equal to  $$ -\frac{2a}{c}+\frac{2a}{b}+\frac{2ai}{b}+\frac{2ai}{c}+\frac{2bi}{a}+\frac{2ci}{a}-\frac{2b}{a}+\frac{2c}{a}+\frac{2b}{c}-\frac{2c}{b}-8i. $$   
Therefore,  $\frac{a-a'}{b'-c'}=-\overline{\left(\frac{a-a'}{b'-c'}\right)}$ , and this proves the claim.

As a result,  $AA',BB',CC'$  must all pass through  $H$ , where  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle A'B'C'$ . In particular, they concur as desired.

Let the square involved in constructing  $A_1$  be  $PQSR$  where  $P$  is on  $AB$ ,  $Q$  is on  $AC$ , and  $R,S$  are on  $BC$ . Triangles  $APQ$  and  $ABC$  are clearly similar. If we let the height from  $A$  to  $BC$  be  $h$  and let the length of  $BC$  be  $a$ , the side length of the square,  $s$ , satisfies  $\frac{s}{a}=\frac{h-s}{h}$  which solves to  $s=\frac{ah}{a+h}$ .
We now employ barycentric coordinates with reference triangle  $ABC$ . We know that  $P=(\frac{a}{a+h},\frac{h}{a+h},0)$  and  $Q=(\frac{a}{a+h},0,\frac{h}{a+h})$ . By EFFT, if  $R=(0,r,1-r)$ , then   $$ \overrightarrow{PR}=(\frac{a}{a+h},\frac{h}{a+h}-r,r-1) $$   and   $$ PR\perp BC\rightarrow 0=a^2(r-1-(\frac{h}{a+h}-r))+(\frac{a}{a+h})(c^2-b^2). $$   This equation solves to 
\begin{align*}
r&=\frac{a^2(-1-\frac{h}{a+h})+(\frac{a}{a+h})(c^2-b^2)}{-2a^2} 
&=\frac{a^2(-(a+h)-h)+(a)(c^2-b^2)}{-2a^2(a+h)} 
&=\frac{c^2-b^2-a^2-2S}{-2a^2-2S} 
&=\frac{2S+a^2+b^2-c^2}{2a^2+2S} 
&=\frac{S+S_C}{a^2+S}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
A_1&=\left(\frac{a}{2(a+h)},\frac{S+S_C}{2(a^2+S)},\frac{h}{2(a+h)}+\frac12-\frac{S+S_C}{2(a^2+S)}\right) 
&=\left(\frac{a}{2(a+h)},\frac{S+S_C}{2a(a+h)},\frac{h}{2(a+h)}+\frac{a(a+h)}{2a(a+h)}-\frac{S+S_C}{2a(a+h)}\right) 
&=\left(a^2:S+S_C:ah+a(a+h)-S-S_C\right) 
&=\left(S_B+S_C:S+S_C:S+S_B\right).
\end{align*}
We claim that the wanted concurrency point is  $(\frac{1}{S+S_A}:\frac{1}{S+S_B}:\frac{1}{S+S_C}).$  We see that line  $AA_1$  is  $-\frac{v}{w}=\frac{z}{y}=\frac{S+S_B}{S+S_C},$  which our concurrency point satisfies. It is easy to show that the concurrency point is also on the other two lines by symmetry. QED.

Let  $A_2$  be the center of the square erected on  $BC$  outside  $ABC$ . So there's a homothety at  $A$  mapping the square centered at  $A_1$  to the one centered at  $A_2$ , so we focus only on ray  $AA_2$ .
