We first note that:   $$ 2\cdot10^{k_1}<\frac{(n+4)!}{(n+1)!}<5\cdot10^{k_1} $$    $$ 1.4\cdot10^{k_2}<\frac{(n+7)!}{(n+4)!}<2\cdot10^{k_2} $$  It suffices to show that for large enough  $n$ ,   $$ 1<\frac{(n+5)(n+6)(n+7)}{(n+2)(n+3)(n+4)}<\frac{14}{5}, $$   and all  $k$  below that large enough  $n$  don't satisfy the wanted. The lower bound is obvious, and the upper bound happens when  $n\ge6$ : 
\begin{align*}
&\frac{(n+5)(n+6)(n+7)}{(n+2)(n+3)(n+4)} 
=&\frac{n+5}{n+2}\cdot\frac{n+6}{n+3}\cdot\frac{n+7}{n+4} 
=&\left(1+\frac{3}{n+2}\right)\cdot\left(1+\frac{3}{n+3}\right)\cdot\left(1+\frac{3}{n+4}\right) 
<&\left(1+\frac{3}{8}\right)^3<(1.4)^3<\frac{14}{5}.
\end{align*}
It can be easily verified that  $n=1,2,3,4,5$  don't work, so we are done. QED.

