It is well known that  $I_A, K, M$  are collinear, where  $I_A$  is the  $A$ -excenter. Hence, we may redefine  $N = \overline{KI_A} \cap (I) \neq D$ . Let  $K'$  be the antipode of  $K$  on  $(I)$ ,  $L$  be the  $A$ -extouch point, and  $T = \overline{D'N} \cap \overline{BC}$ . By a well-known lemma,  $A, K', L$  are collinear.

We claim that  $(BC;KT) = -1$ . Indeed, this is equivalent to 
\begin{align*}
	\frac{TC}{TB} &= \frac{KC}{KB} 
	\frac{TK - KC}{TK + KB} &= \frac{KC}{KB} 
	\iff TK \cdot KB - KC \cdot KB &= TK \cdot KC + KC \cdot KB 
	\iff TK(KB - KC) &= 2KB \cdot KC. 
\end{align*}

Then, note that  $\triangle KLI_A \sim \triangle KNT \sim \triangle K'KT$ . Hence, we have
\begin{align*}
	\frac{LK}{KK'} &= \frac{I_AL}{TK} 
	TK &= \frac{I_AL \cdot KK'}{LK}.
\end{align*}

Now, let  $a, b, c, r, s$  have their customary meanings, and additionally define  $r_A$  to be the radius of the  $A$ -excircle. Note that
\begin{align*}
	KB &= s - b 
	KC = BL &= s - c 
	KK' &= 2r 
	I_AL &= r_A.
\end{align*}
In addition, 
\begin{align*}
	LK &= BC - BL - CK 
	&= a - 2(s - c) 
	&= c - b 
	&= (s - b) - (s - c) 
	&= KB - KC.
\end{align*}
Therefore, noting the well-known  $r_A = \frac{s}{s - a}r$ , we have
\begin{align*}
	(BC;KT) &= -1 
	\iff TK(KB - KC) &= 2KB \cdot KC 
	\iff \frac{I_AL \cdot KK'}{LK} (KB - KC) &= 2(s - b)(s - c) 
	\iff \frac{r_A \cdot 2r}{KB - KC} (KB - KC) &= 2(s - b)(s - c) 
	\iff r_A \cdot r &= (s - b)(s - c) 
	\iff \frac{s}{s - a}r^2 &= (s - b)(s - c) 
	\iff sr^2 &= (s - a)(s - b)(s - c) 
	\iff s^2r^2 &= s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c),
\end{align*}
which is true as both sides are equal to  $[ABC]^2$ . Thus,  $(BC;KT) = -1$ , as claimed. 

Since  $\angle KNT = 90^\circ$ , it follows from the right angles and bisectors lemma that  $\overline{NK}$  bisects  $\angle BKC$ . Therefore, as  $(I)$  is tangent to  $\overline{BC}$ , a chord of  $(BKC)$ , by Archimedes Lemma it follows that  $(I)$  is internally tangent to  $(BKC)$ . This completes the proof.  $\Box$

Let  $N' \in \Omega $  such that the circumcircle of  $\triangle BN'C$  (name it  $\Gamma$ ) is tangent to  $\Omega$ .  $ N'K$  passes through  $J$  the midpoint of arc  $BC$  in circle  $\Gamma$  .
Invert about  $K$  with power  $-KB \cdot KC$  . Call this map  $\Phi$ . $\Gamma$  stays fixed under this map. Let  $\omega$  be the circumcircle of  $\triangle BIC$ . Easy to see that  $\omega$  stays fixed under  $\Phi$ . $\Phi : N' \leftrightarrow J$  . So  $\Omega$  inverts to a line  $l$  tangent to  $\Gamma$  at  $J$  .   $KI$  meet  $\Omega$  again at  $L$  . Let  $S$  and  $F$  be the inverses of  $I$  and  $L$ , respectively.  $KL \cdot KF = KI \cdot KS = \frac{KL}{2} \cdot KS \implies KS=2KF$  . Let  $T$  be the reflection of  $K$  about the midpoint of  $BC$ . From the fact that  $I_aT \perp BC$ ,  $ISI_a = 90^{\circ}$ , and  $SK \perp BC$  we get that  $TKSI_a$  is a rectangle. So if we let  $E$  be the midpoint of  $TI_a$  then  $E$  lies on  $l$  and  $J$  is the midpoint of  $FE$ . Hence the diagonals of  $TKSI_a$  intersect at  $J$ , i.e.  $K$ ,  $J$ , and  $I_a$  are collinear. Since  $N'$  is the inverse of  $J$  we get that it lies on  $KJ$ . So  $N'$ ,  $K$ ,  $J$ , and  $I_a$  are collinear.   It's well known that  $M$ ,  $K$ , and  $I_a$  are collinear. So  $N'$  is in fact the intersection of  $MK$  with  $\Omega$ . So  $N=N'$  and we get the desired conclusion.  $\blacksquare$ [asy] 
