Let  $L,P$  be the intersections of incircle with  $\overline{AC}$  and  $\overline{AB}$ . Let  $E,F,G,H$  be midpoints of  $BK,BP,CK,CL$ . Let  $J \equiv \overline{EF}\cap\overline{GH}$ . Let  $I_A$  be the excenter and  $Z,Z'$  be foots of altitude from  $J,I_A$  onto  $\overline{BC}$ . We present the following claim.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span> Points  $J,K,H$  are collinear.
*Proof.* Assume  $H' \equiv \overline{JK}\cap\overline{AD}$ . Also we clearly have  $DH=c\cdot\sin(\angle B)/2~(\clubsuit)$ . Next, since  $EG=BC/2$  and  $\overline{EJ}\parallel\overline{BI_A}, \overline{GJ}\parallel\overline{CI_A}$  we can conclude  $\triangle EJH \sim \triangle BI_AC$  with side-length ratio  $2$ . Therefore,  $JZ=JZ'/2=r_A/2$ . Note that  $\triangle JKZ \sim \triangle H'KD$  implying   $$ \frac{JZ}{H'D}=\frac{KZ}{KD} \implies \frac{r_A/2}{H'D}=\frac{(a/2)-(s-b)}{s-b-c\cdot\cos(\angle B)}\implies H'D=\frac{r_A \cdot [s-b-c\cdot\cos(\angle B)]}{b-c} $$   Now  $H'D = HD$  iff 
\begin{align*}
\frac{r_A \cdot [s-b-c \cdot \cos(\angle B)]}{b-c} &= \frac{c \cdot \sin(\angle B)}{2} 
\iff \frac{\cancel{\Delta} \cdot [s-b-c \cdot \cos(\angle B)]}{(s-a) \cdot (b-c)} &= \frac{\cancel{\Delta}}{a} 
\iff \frac{s-c-c \cdot \cos(\angle B)}{b-c} &= \frac{s}{a}  
\iff a(s-c) - ac\left(\frac{2s(s-b)}{ac} - 1\right) &= s(b-c) 
\iff sa - ac - (a+b+c)(s-b) + ac &= sb - sc 
\iff \cancel{sa} - \cancel{ac} - (\cancel{sa}+sb+\cancel{sc}) + (ab + b^2 + cb) + \cancel{ac} &= sb - \cancel{sc} 
\iff b(a+b+c) &= 2sb 
\iff a+b+c &=2s 
\end{align*}
which is true. Therefore, we can conclude  $H \equiv H'\implies J,K,H$  are collinear, as required.  $\square$ Denote the  $\angle HKD = \theta$ . It is worth noting that  $\angle HKD = \angle ZKJ$  for later calculation. Let  $X$  be the cirumcenter of  $\odot(BJC)$ . We present another claim now (expected).

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span> Points  $X,I,N$  are collinear.
*Proof.* For this we first find the value of  $NK$ . Note that  $\angle NIK = 2\angle NLK = 2\angle NKD = 2\theta$ . Applying Law of Sines in  $\triangle NIK,$  we get   $$ \frac{NK}{\sin(2\theta)} = \frac{r}{\sin(90^\circ - \theta)} \implies NK = 2r\cdot \sin(\theta)= \left(\frac{r \cdot r_A}{\sqrt{(\frac{b-c}{2})^2 + (\frac{r_A}{2})^2}}\right) $$  Also note that  $JX$  is the circumradius for  $\odot(BJC),$  so using the well known formula  $R=abc/4\Delta$  it is nothing but   $$ JX = \frac{a \cdot \sqrt{(\frac{r_A}{2})^2 + (\frac{a}{2})^2} \cdot \sqrt{(\frac{r_A}{2})^2 + (\frac{a}{2})^2}}{4 \cdot \left(\frac{1}{2} \cdot a \cdot \frac{r_A}{2}\right)}=\frac{r_A^2 + a^2}{4r_A} $$  Now it suffices to show that  $JN/KN=JX/KI$  but then this is true iff  $JK/KN = (JX-KI)/KI$ . Now this is true 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sqrt{(\frac{b-c}{2})^2 + (\frac{r_A}{2})^2}}{r \cdot r_A \div \sqrt{(\frac{b-c}{2})^2 + (\frac{r_A}{2})^2}} &= \frac{r_A^2 + a^2/4r_A - r}{r}
\iff \frac{(\frac{b-c}{2})^2 + (\frac{r_A}{2})^2}{\cancel{r \cdot r_A}} &= \frac{r_A^2 + a^2 - 4r_A \cdot r}{4\cancel{r_A\cdot r}} 
\iff (b-c)^2+\cancel{r_A^2} &=\cancel{r_A^2}+a^2-4r_A \cdot r 
\iff 4(s-b)(s-c)&=(a-b+c)(a+b-c)~~\left(\because r=\frac{\Delta}{a}, r_A=\frac{\Delta}{s-a} \right)
\iff 4(s-b)(s-c)&=(2s-2b)(2s-2c)=4(s-b)(s-c)
\end{align*}
which is true. This eastablishes that  $X,I,N$  are collinear. \square
Now, it suffices to show that  $BJCN$  is cyclic. Now this quadrilateral will be cyclic iff 
\begin{align*}
BK\cdot KC &= JK \cdot KN 
\iff (s-b) \cdot (s-c) &=\left(\sqrt{(\tfrac{b-c}{2})^2+(\tfrac{r_A}{2})^2}\right) \cdot \frac{r \cdot r_A}{\left(\sqrt{(\frac{b-c}{2})^2+(\frac{r_A}{2})^2}\right)} 
\iff (s-b) \cdot (s-c) &= r \cdot r_A = (s-b)(s-c) 
\end{align*}
which is true. Since we have shown that four points, namely  $B,J,C,N$  are concyclic and that centers of  $\odot(BNC),\odot(I)$  and  $N$  are collinear, showing  $\odot(BNC)$  tangent to  $\odot(I)$  at  $N$ . This finishes the problem.  $\blacksquare$

