*Proof:* \begin{align*}2NK\cdot KE&=NK\cdot KI_A&=II_A ^2-KI_A ^2-r^2&=(r+r_a)^2+KF^2-KF^2-r_a ^2-r^2=2rr_a&=2r^2\frac{s}{s-a}&=2(s-b)(s-c)&=2BK\cdot KC,\end{align*} by definition, PoP, Pythagoras, Lemma 2.19, Heron, and Lemma 2.15, respectively. Thus, by PoP, we are done.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  $E$  is the midpoint of  $\widehat{BC}.$ *Proof:* Let  $X$  be the foot from  $E$  to  $\overline{BC}.$  Notice that  $\triangle KXE\sim\triangle KFI_A,$  and since  $E$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{KI_A},$   $X$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{KF}$  and therefore  $\overline{BC},$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$ We are done by Lemma 4.33.  $\square$ *Note: When using lemmas, we are referring to EGMO.*

Let  $P,Q$  be the tangent point on  $AC,AB$  with  $\Omega$  respectively. We use converse of Casey's theorem on the circles  $\Omega$  and degenerate circles  $(B), (C), (N)$ . It is suffice to prove that
\begin{eqnarray*}
BC\cdot 0+BK\cdot CN&=&BN\cdot CK
\implies \frac{BK}{CK}&=&\frac{BN}{CN}
\end{eqnarray*}

This means we have to prove  $NK$  bisects  $\angle BNC$ .

Let  $K'$  be the antipode of  $K$  wrt  $\Omega$ . Let  $F:=KN\cap PQ$ . Applying power of point theorem we have
\begin{eqnarray*}
FN\cdot FK'=FQ\cdot FP&=&FI^2-r^2
&=&FK^2\hspace{5mm} \text{(by Pythagoras)}
\end{eqnarray*}

which gives that  $F$  lies on  $BC$ . Since the lines  $AK,BP,CQ$  concur at the Georgonne point, therefore we can say  $(B,C;K,F)=-1$ . And  $FK'\perp NK$  because our assumption  $K'$  was the antipode of  $K$ . Hence we have  $NK$  bisects  $\angle BNC$ .

