The smallest such  $t$  is  $t=\boxed{4}$ .  We prove this assertion with the 3 following claims.**Claim 1**:  $x_i^3\equiv \{-1,0,1\}\pmod{9}$ .

*Proof*: We check each residue modulo  $9$ .  We have
\begin{align*}
0^3&\equiv 0\pmod{9}
1^3&\equiv 1\pmod{9}
2^3&\equiv -1\pmod{9}
3^3&\equiv 0\pmod{9}
4^3&\equiv 1\pmod{9}
5^3&\equiv -1\pmod{9}
6^3&\equiv 0\pmod{9}
7^3&\equiv 1\pmod{9}
8^3&\equiv -1\pmod{9}
\end{align*}
which means all perfect cubes  $x_i^3\equiv \{-1,0,1\}\pmod{9}$  as desired  $\blacksquare$ **Claim 2**:  $t\ge 4$  is minimal.

*Proof*: We once again look at the given information under modulo  $9$ .  We see that   $$ 2002^{2002}\equiv 4^{2002}\equiv 4\left(\underbrace{4^{3}\cdot 4^{3}\cdots 4^{3}\cdot 4^{3}}_{667~\text{many times}}\right)\equiv 4\pmod{9}. $$  This implies that  $t\ge 4$  is minimal since all residues are either  $-1,0$  or  $1$  modulo  $9$  which means we must have at least  $4$  such  $x_i$  , completing the proof  $\blacksquare$ **Claim 3**:  $t=4$  works.

*Proof*: We finish with a construction.  All we need to do is note that
\begin{align*}
2002^{2002}&=2002\left(2002^{667}\right)^3
&=\left(10\cdot 2002^{667}\right)^3+\left(10\cdot 2002^{667}\right)^3+\left(2002^{667}\right)^3+\left(2002^{667}\right)^3
\end{align*}
so our construction is complete, finishing our work  $\blacksquare$

