The answer is  $f(n)=n$  which obviously works; now we show this is the only solution. We have  $f(1)^2+f(1) \mid 4 \Rightarrow f(1)=1$ . Now for any prime  $p$  we have
\begin{align*}
1+f(p-1) &\mid p^2 
f(p-1)^2+1 &\mid ((p-1)^2+1)^2 
\end{align*}
The first equation tells us  $f(p-1) \in \{ p-1, p^2-1 \}$ . If  $f(p-1)=p^2-1$  then the second equation yields a contradiction for large enough  $p$  (say, by expanding and euclidean algorithm). Thus  $f(p-1)=p-1$  for large enough  $p$ ; in particular,  $f$  has arbitrarily large fixed points. For any  $n$ , let  $M$  be a fixed point. Then   $$ M^2+f(n) \mid (M^2+n)^2-(M^2+f(n))(M^2+2n-f(n)) = (f(n)-n)^2 $$   and by making  $M$  fat we win.

