Let  $EF \cap DC = P, G = AB \cap CD, J = PA \cap BC$ . 

Then by Brokard's theorem on  $ABCD$  and  $AFCP$ ,  $(D, C; P, G) = (J, B; F, C) = -1$  are harmonic bundles. Observe also that  $ANBM$  is harmonic (and completely unrelated to the problem, that  $MN$  is the symmedian of  $MAB$  and  $NAB$ ).**Claim:**  $P$  lies on  $(ANBM)$ . **Proof:** We have  $GA \cdot GB = GD \cdot GC = (GM - MD)(GM + MD) = GM^2 - MD^2$ . Therefore it suffices to show  $GP \cdot GM = GM^2 - MD^2$ : As  $(D, C; P, G) = -1$ , 
\begin{align*}
\frac{GC}{CP} &= \frac{GD}{DP} 
\frac{GM + MD}{MD + PM} &= \frac{GM - MD}{MD - PM} 
\implies \frac{GM}{MD} &= \frac{MD}{MP} 
\implies GM \cdot MP &= MD^2 
\implies GM \cdot GP &= GM^2 - MD^2, \text{as desired.} \square
\end{align*}

