We first prove the following lemma:**Lemma.** If there are  $E$  edges and  $T$  triangles in a graph, then  $T\le\frac{\sqrt2}{3} E^{3/2}$ .**Proof.** Because
\begin{align*}
T &\le \frac13\sum_{v\in V}\min\left(E,\binom{\deg v}{2}\right) 
&\le \frac13\sum_{v\in V}\sqrt{E}\cdot\frac{\deg v}{\sqrt2} 
&= \frac{\sqrt2}{3}E^{3/2}.
\end{align*}

Back to the main problem. We claim the answer is  $\frac{3}{32}$ ; necessity is shown by complete graphs. Now we prove sufficiency. Let  $c_1=\frac{\sqrt2}{3}$ ,  $A=f(G)$ ,  $B=g(G)$ . Let  $d_T(v)$  be the number of triangles with one vertex  $v$ . The number of tetrahedrons with one vertex  $v$  is the same as the number of triangles in the induced subgraph on  $v$ 's neighbors, which by the lemma is  $\le c_1 d_T(v)^{3/2}$  since  $d_T(v)$  is the number of edges in the induced subgraph on  $v$ 's neighbors.

Now note that
\begin{align*}
B &\le \frac14\sum_{v\in V}\min\left(A,c_1 d_T(v)^{3/2}\right) 
&\le \frac14\sum_{v\in V}A^{1/3}c^{2/3}d_T(v) 
&=\frac34c^{2/3}\cdot A^{4/3} 
&=\left(\frac{3}{32}\right)^{1/3}\cdot A^{4/3},
\end{align*}
as desired.  $\blacksquare$

We claim that  $C=\tfrac{N}{2},$  achieved by splitting the matrix into the four equal square submatrices  $\begin{pmatrix}0 & 1  -1 & 0\end{pmatrix}.$ Note that we can permute the rows and columns such that only the first  $w$  columns and the first  $y$  columns have positive sum. Let  $x=N-w,z=N-y.$  Let the corresponding sums of the entries in each region be  $W,X,Y,Z$  as shown:

