<details><summary>solution</summary>We consider a graph  $G=(V,E)$ . Also let  $f(n)$  be the number of labellings in (b).
(a)**<span style="color:#741B47">Claim:</span>**  $r_n\geq n-1$ **<span style="color:#A64D79">Proof:</span>** For  $n=3$  we clearly have  $r_3=2$ . Suppose by induction that  $r_{n-1}=n-2$ . Consider one the possible labellings for the  $n-1$  vertices and add a new vertex now. Label all edges from this new vertex to the other  $n-1$  vertices with  $n-1$ . This clearly satisfies (1) and (2).  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#741B47">Claim:</span>** If we have a labelling for  $n$  vertices that satisfies (2) but not necessarily (1), we can shift the numbers s.t. they satisfy (1).**<span style="color:#A64D79">Proof:</span>** Consider  $\{a,a+1,\dots,a+k_a,b,b+1,\dots,b+k_b,c,c+1,\dots,c+k_c,\dots\}$  are the numbers used for the labelling satisfying (2). Shifting 
\begin{align*}
\{a,a+1,\dots,a+k_a,b,b+1,\dots,b+k_b,\dots\} &\to  \{1,\dots,k_a,b,b+1,\dots,b+k_b,\dots\} 
&\to \{1,\dots,k_a,k_a+1,\dots,k_a+k_b+1,\dots\} 
&\to \cdots
\end{align*}
shows that if (2) is satisfied, we can change the numbers s.t. (1) is satisfied.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#741B47">Claim:</span>**  $r_n\leq n-1$ **<span style="color:#A64D79">Proof:</span>** Suppose that  $r_n\geq n$ . Consider a vertex  $v$  and delete it. Then the remaining  $n-1$  vertices satisfy (2). By shifting using claim 2 and induction, we know they only use at most  $n-2$  different numbers for the labels. Thus  $v$  had edges with at least  $2$  new numbers used for its labels. Let these edges be  $vu, vw$  with  $u,w$  vertices among the remaining  $n-1$  vertices. However, then  $vu, vw$  and  $uw$  all have different numbers for their labels ( $uw$  cannot have the same number as  $vu,vw$  as the numbers for these to edges are new!). Thus (2) is not satisfied and hence  $r_n\leq n-1$ . 
With claim 1, this shows  $r_n=n-1$ .  $\blacksquare$ (b)**<span style="color:#741B47">Claim:</span>** We can partition our graph into  $X$  and  $Y$  s.t. only and all edges  $uv$  with  $u\in X$  and  $v\in Y$  are labelled with  $n-1$ .**<span style="color:#A64D79">Proof:</span>** Suppose we vertices  $u\in X,v\in Y$  with edge  $uv$  labelled  $r_n$ . Then for any third vertex  $w$ , as  $n-1$  is the largest possible number, either  $uw$  or  $vw$  is labelled with  $n-1$ . If it is  $uw$ , then let  $w\in Y$ , if it is  $vw$  then let  $w\in X$ . This gives the result.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#741B47">Claim:</span>**  $f(n)=\frac{n!(n-1)!}{2^{n-1}}$ **<span style="color:#A64D79">Proof:</span>** Notice that by our claim above, we want to choose some  $i$  vertices in  $X$  and the other  $n-i$  vertices in  $Y$ . Moreover, by claim 2 from (a), we want to choose  $i-1$  numbers from the available  $n-2$  numbers to use in the labelling of  $X$  (note we cannot choose  $n-1$ ). For  $X$  we then have  $f(i)$  possible labellings and for  $Y$  we have  $f(n-i)$  labellings. Do this for  $i=1,\dots,n-1$  (which double-counts) to get all possible labellings and therefore
\begin{align*}
    f(n)&=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}{n\choose i}{n-2 \choose i-1}f(i)f(n-i)
    &=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\frac{n!}{i!(n-i)!}\cdot\frac{(n-2)!}{(i-1)!(n-i-1)!}f(i)f(n-i)  
\end{align*}
Define  $g(i):=\frac{f(i)}{i!(i-1)!}$  and suppose by induction that  $g(i)=\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}$ . Then   $$ f(n)=n!(n-1)!g(n)=\frac{1}{2}n!(n-2)!\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}g(i)g(n-i) $$  indeed holds as   $$ (n-1)g(n)=\frac{n-1}{2^{n-1}}=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\cdot \frac{1}{2^{n-i-1}}=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}g(i)g(n-i) \, . $$  So  $f(n)=\frac{n!(n-1)!}{2^{n-1}}$ .  $\blacksquare$ This completes the problem.  $\square$</details>

