Let  $M_A$  be the midpoint of minor arc  $\widehat{BC}$  in  $\odot (ABC),$  and let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $DE.$  Also let  $r,R,a,b,c$  have their usual meanings.

First, we note that  $PP'\parallel DE$  so it suffices to show that  $\delta(I,DE)=\delta(I,PP').$ **Lemma:**  $PP'$  is the  $F$ -midline of  $\triangle FDE.$ **Proof:** Inversion about the incircle.

Now it clearly follows that  $\delta(I,DE)=\delta(I,PP')\iff \delta(I,DE)=\frac{1}{4}\delta(F,DE).$ **Lemma:**  $\delta(I,DE)=\frac{r^2}{IA}.$ **Proof:**We compute  $\delta(I,DE)$  by noting that   $$ \triangle IEM\sim \triangle IAE\implies \delta(I,DE)=IM=\frac{r^2}{IA}.\quad (1) $$  **Lemma:**  $\delta(F,DE)=\frac{r\cdot IA}{R}.$ **Proof:**We compute  $\delta(F,DE)$  as  $\frac{[FDE]}{DM}.$  We compute these separately:   $$ [FDE]=[FDI]+[DEI]+[EFI]\stackrel{\text{sin area}}{=}\frac{1}{2}r^2(\sin A+\sin B+\sin C)\stackrel{\text{b+c=3a}}{=}\frac{r^2}{4R}(a+b+c)=\frac{r^2a}{R} $$  and  $$ MD=\sqrt{MI\cdot MA}=\sqrt{MI\cdot(AI-MI)}\stackrel{\text{(1)}}{=}\frac{r}{IA}\sqrt{IA^2-r^2}=\frac{ra}{IA}. $$  Then  $\delta(F,DE)=\frac{r\cdot IA}{R}.$ From  $\delta(I,DE)=\delta(I,PP')\iff \delta(I,DE)=\frac{1}{4}\delta(F,DE)$  and substitution, it suffices to show  $4rR=IA^2.$  But Euler's  $OI$  theorem states that  $2rR=\text{pow}(I,\odot(ABC))=IA\cdot IM_A.$  Thus it suffices to show  $IA=2IM_A,$  which follows from applying Ptolemy to  $ABM_AC$  and using  $b+c=3a$  as well as the fact that  $M_A$  is the center of  $\odot (BIC).$

**Lemma:** Let  $ABC$  be a triangle where  $E$  is the tangency point of A-excircle,  $N$  is the Nagel point and  $R$  is the intersection (closest to  $A$ ) of  $AE$  with the incircle of  $ABC$ . Then  $AR = NE$ .**Proof:** Let  $I$  be the incenter of triangle  $ABC$ ,  $G$  the centroid of triangle  $ABC$ ,  $M$  the midpoint of  $BC$  and  $D$  the in-touch point with  $BC$ . Then, since  $M$  is the midpoint of  $DE$  (a well known fact), the line  $IM$  is the midsegment of triangle  $EKD$ , so  $2IM=ER$ . Moreover, the homothety centered at  $G$  and scale factor -2 maps  $I$  to  $N$  and  $M$  to  $A$ . Hence  $AN=2IM=ER$  and it follows that  $AR=NE$ .

Denote by  $M$  and  $N$  the midpoints of the segments  $AC$  and  $BC$ ,  by  $T$  the tangency point of C-excircle with the side  $AB$  and by  $\omega$  the incircle of  $\Delta ABC$ . We have:
\begin{align*}
AB+NM=AB+\frac{1}{2}AB=\frac{3}{2}AB=\frac{1}{2}AC+\frac{1}{2}BC=AM+BN,
\end{align*}
so by Pitot theorem,  $\omega$  is tangent to  $MN$  at some point  $P$ . Homothety centered at  $C$  mapping  $\omega$  to the C-excircle maps  $P$  to  $T$  thus points  $C, P, T$  are collinear. This means that  $CP$  is the nagelian of the triangle  $ABC$ , so from **Lemma** it follows that  $P$  is its midpoint. Analogously, we deduce that  $AK$  and  $BL$  are also nagelians of triangle  $ABC$ , so they meet at  $P$ . Hence, because lines  $AB$  and  $MN$  are parallel we obtain:
\begin{align*}
\sphericalangle KAB = \sphericalangle PAB = \sphericalangle MPA = \sphericalangle MPK = \sphericalangle KLP = 180^{\circ} - \sphericalangle KLB,
\end{align*}
therefore  $KLBA$  is cyclic.

