f(x+1)+f(x)f(1)&=f(x)+2x+1
\end{align}
Hence,  $f(x+1)=2x+1$ . Thus,  $\boxed{f(x)=2x-1}$  for all real  $x$ , and it is easy to verify this works. Now we visit the case when  $f(1)=0$ . Note  $(1)$  transforms to   $$ f(x+1)=f(x)+2x+1\implies f(x+1)-(x+1)^2=f(x)-x^2. $$  A simple double induction proves that  $f(x+n)=f(x)+2nx+n^2$  for all integers  $n$ . Another induction shows that  $f(n)=n^2-1$ . Taking  $y=n$  for any integer  $n$ , we see   $$ f(nx)=f(x+n)+f(x)f(n)-2nx-1 = f(x)+2nx+n^2+(n^2-1)f(x)-2nx-1=n^2f(x)+n^2-1. $$  Note that taking  $n=-1$  gives  $f(-x)=f(x)$ . Plugging  $y=x$  gives 
\begin{align*}\tag{2}
f(x^2)+2x^2+1=f(2x)+f(x)^2=f(x)^2+4f(x)+3
\end{align*}
Now taking  $y=-x$  grants \begin{align*}\tag{3} f(0)+f(x)f(-x)=f(-x^2)-2x^2+1\implies f(x)^2=f(x^2)-2x^2+2\end{align*}
Combining  $(2)$  and  $(3)$  gives  $2x^2+1-(4f(x)+3)=-2x^2+2$ . Therefore  $\boxed{f(x)=x^2-1}$ . **f(2)=-3** In this case  $f(1)^2-f(1)-6=0$ , so that  $f(1)=3$  or  $f(1)=-2$ . Suppose  $f(1)=3$ . Trying  $y=1$  grants   $$ f(x+1)=-2f(x)+2x+1. $$   Computing, we see  $f(3)=11$ ,  $f(5)=39$  and  $f(6)=-67$ . Now taking  $(x,y)=(2,3)$  gives   $$ f(5)+f(2)f(3)=f(6)+13\implies f(6)=-7, $$   which does not match with  $f(6)=-67$ . Thus, we must have  $f(1)=-2$ . Again, we take  $y=1$  to get   $$ f(x+1)=3f(x)+2x+1. $$   A little bashing gives  $f(x-1)=(f(x)-2x+1)/3$ . Note  $(x,-1)$  grants   $$ f(-x)=2x-1+f(x-1)=2x-1+(f(x)-2x+1)/3. $$  Replacing  $x$  with  $-x$  in the above equation and adding gives  $f(x)+f(-x)=-2$ . Plugging  $f(-x)=-2-f(x)$  back into the equation gives   $$ -2-f(x)=2x-1+(f(x)-2x+1)/3. $$   Solving, we get  $\boxed{f(x)=-x-1}$ . We can easily verify all the derived solutions work.

