We get to the fun part --- the length chase! (as shown here, it's very misleading to say that a short and simple sol automatically means that the problem is short and simple.) 

First, note that 
\[ (A,Z;K,L) = -1 \]
since  $AE, AF$  tangent to  $(LEFK)$ .  $\blacksquare$  
--------
Next, notice that the problem is equivalent to showing  $MQ = MP$ . Furthermore, 
\[ \dfrac{MQ}{LY} = \dfrac{AM}{AL} \]
and  $\frac{MP}{KX} = \frac{AM}{AK}$ , its counterpart. Also note that
\[ \dfrac{LY}{KX} = \dfrac{LZ}{ZK} \]
since  $LK,ID$  concur at  $Z$  implies  $LK,XY,ID,AM$  to concur at  $Z$ , too (since  $XY$  is the reflection of  $KL$  to  $ID$ ).  $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$  
--------
Since all we have are ratios, it might be good to restate  $MQ = MP$  into
\[ \dfrac{MQ}{MP} = 1. \]
Then,
\[ \dfrac{MQ}{MP} = \dfrac{\frac{AM}{AL} \cdot LY}{\frac{AM}{AK} \cdot KX} = \dfrac{LY \cdot AK}{AL \cdot KX} = \dfrac{LZ}{ZK} \cdot \dfrac{AK}{AL} = 1, \] 
the last line being true because of  $(A,Z;K,L) = -1$ . 

We are done.  $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$  
--------
<details><summary>Motivation: Projective and inversion freezing on the spot? Try computing lengths and ratios!</summary>As the Motivation title suggests, I tried inversion with respect to the incircle (after realizing  $AM, EF, DI$ , and in turn,  $AM,EF,DI,XY$  concur). 

I wanted to preserve the cross ratio of  $(L,K;E,F)$  and tranforme that with  $A,Z$  or some other point to get
\[ (B,D;Q,C) = (C,E;P,B) \]
with  $E$  being the intersection of  $A,D^{\text{antipode}}$  with  $BC$ . I was very unsuccessful with this attempt, so I also tried to use what I often call  $\textsf{the six-point harmonic projection}$  or
\[ A,B,C,D,E,F \ \text{concylic}; \ AB,CD,EF \ \text{concur} \ \Rightarrow (A,C;D,E) = (B,D;C,F) \]
but I didn't know how to construct the respective  $A$  to  $F$ . 

From this, I concluded that the condition  $AE,AF$  tangent plays a big role to prove  $BP = CQ$ . This hastily led me to look at  $K$  and  $L$  more (as they're the symmedian intersections of the two tangents with the incircle). 

So, looking explicitly to the ratio of  $\frac{AK}{AL}$  in said harmonic ratio, I wanted to project  $KM_K$  and  $LM_L$  to  $BC$ , with  $M_K,M_L$  (as its name suggests) is the midpoint of  $KX, YL$ . 

This turned out to be very computable, and it turns out that  $M_K,M_L$  are unneeded, as I could just compare  $LY$  and  $KX$  in the first place with the other (still unused) ratio
\[ \dfrac{LZ}{ZK}  \]
from the harmonic ratio  $(A,Z;K,L) = -1$  (where we have used the ratio  $\frac{AK}{AL}$  earlier to  $\textit{project}$  the parallels to  $BC$ ). 
