Let  $\Gamma$  denote the incircle. We define a couple points:


- Let  $D = BC \cap \Gamma$  and define  $E$  and  $F$  similarly.
- Let  $G = DI \cap EF$

It is well known that  $G$  lies on  $AM$ . Then it is easy to see that  $KX \perp DI$  and  $YL \perp DI$ , such that  $KXLY$  is an isosceles trapezoid that is symmetric about  $DI$ . Therefore we must have  $G$  lie on  $XY$ . 

Now we will generalize the problem statement. Let  $X$  be any point on  $\Gamma$ , and let  $Y = XG \cap \Gamma$ . Let  $P = AX \cap BC$  and  $Q = AY \cap BC$ . Then we will show that we must have  $BP = CQ$ . Note that it suffices to show that  $M$  is the midpoint of  $PQ$ . 

Let  $\ell$  denote the line through  $A$  parallel to  $BC$ . We claim that  $\ell$  is the polar of  $G$  with respect to the incircle. To see this note that  $A$  is tangent to  $\Gamma$  at  $E$  and  $F$  and that  $GI \perp \ell$ . Thus if  $H = XY \cap \ell$ , we have  $(XY,HG) = -1$ . Now to finish note that,
\begin{align*}
    -1 = (XY,HG) \overset{A}{=} (QP,M\infty)
\end{align*}
where  $\infty$  denotes the point at infinity along  $\ell$ . Thus  $M$  is indeed the midpoint of  $PQ$  and thus  $BP = CQ$ .

