The answer is  $1$  and all primes. We first show necessity, then prove the construction at the end. The key claim is the following:**<span style="color:#00f">Claim.</span>** If  $p$  is a prime divisor of  $n$ , then  $\nu_p(n!) = 1$ .
Assume otherwise. Fix some prime  $p$  dividing  $n$  with  $\nu_p(n!) > 1$ . Under this assumption, there must exist some element  $g$  of order  $p$  modulo  $p^{\nu_p(n!)}$ . So we may go ahead and construct the positive integer  $m$  such that
\begin{align*}
m &\equiv g \pmod{p^{\nu_p(n!)}} \text{ and} 
m &\equiv 1 \pmod{q^{\nu_q(n!)}}
\end{align*}
for any other prime  $q \mid n!$ . By construction,  $m^n \equiv (m^p)^{n/p} \equiv 1 \pmod{n!}$ .

Now for the contradiction: suppose we have some residue  $a$  satisfying  $a^n \equiv -1 \pmod{n!}$ . Then
\[(am)^n \equiv a^nm^n \equiv a^n \equiv -1 \pmod{n!}.\]
By uniqueness,  $am \equiv a \pmod{n!} \iff m \equiv 1 \pmod{n!}$ , which is absurd.  $\square$ Therefore, any prime dividing  $n$  must be strictly greater than  $\frac{n}{2}$ , so  $n = 1$  or  $n$  is prime itself. To prove the construction, note that  $1$  and  $2$  trivially work, and if  $n = p$  is an odd prime then
\[a^p \equiv -1 \pmod{p!} \implies a^{2p} \equiv 1 \pmod {p!}\]

so  $a$  has order  $1, 2, p,$  or  $2p$ .  $1$  does not work by inspection, and  $p$  cannot divide the order because  $p \nmid \varphi(p!)$ . Thus,  $a \equiv -1$ , which can be checked to work.  $\square$

I claim that the answer is  $1$  and all primes. 

The result is obvious for  $1,$  so I will now show that for all primes  $p,$  there exists a unique integer  $a \in \{1, 2, \ldots, p!\}$  with  $p! \mid a^p+1.$  

Since  $p$  is prime, observe that  $p \nmid \varphi(p!),$  so there exists some odd constant  $k$  such that  $kp \equiv 1 \pmod{\varphi(p!)}.$  Raising both sides of the congruence   $$ a^p \equiv -1 \pmod{p!} $$   to the power of that constant  $k,$  we get
\begin{align*}
    a^{pk} \equiv -1 \pmod{p!} &\implies a \equiv -1 \pmod{p!}.
\end{align*}
Therefore,  $p$  is forced to be equal to  $p!-1,$  and it is not difficult to check that this value of  $a$  indeed works, so the desired result holds true. 

Now, it remains to show that for all positive integers  $n>1$  such that  $n$  isn't prime, there either no exists no  $a \in \{1, 2, \ldots, n!\}$  such that  $n! \mid a^n+1$  or there exists two or more such values of  $a.$  I proceed with casework on whether  $n$  is even or  $n$  is odd (and composite).

If  $n>2$  is even, then note that if there is some  $a \in \{1, 2, \ldots, n!\} \setminus \{n!/2\}$  such that  $n! \mid a^n+1,$  then  $n!-a$  is another such value, and it is distinct. It is not difficult to see that for all  $n>2,$  we have
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{n!}{2}\right)^n+1 &\equiv 1 
&\not\equiv 0 \pmod{n!},
\end{align*}
so all desired values  $a$  indeed come in pairs. This implies that either there cannot exist exactly one such value of  $a,$  as claimed. 

If  $n$  is an odd composite, then I claim that  $a=n!-1$  and  $a=(n-1)!-1$  are two desired values. The former is obviously valid, and for the latter, since  $n$  is an odd composite, we have  $n \mid (n-1)!,$  so it results by the Binomial Theorem that
\begin{align*}
    ((n-1)!-1)^n &\equiv \sum_{k=0}^n \binom{n}{k}(n-1)!^k (-1)^{n-k} 
    &\equiv (-1)^n 
    &\equiv -1 \pmod{n!}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, there exists more than one desired value of  $a,$  as claimed. 

Having exhausted all cases, we are done.  $\square$

