Let the  $A-$  altitude in  $\triangle ABC$  be  $A_1$  and similarly let the  $B,C$  altitudes be  $B_1$  and  $C_1$  respectively 

[Using Property 2 in this post from the great Math Pi Rate's blog](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c701535h1754217) It suffices to show that the circles  $\odot(ADE)$   $\odot(ABC)$   $\odot(AH)$  have a common point (The Queue point)
We will show that the Queue point =  $Q$  lies on  $\odot(ADE)$ To do this we invert with center  $A$  and radius  $r = \sqrt{AH \cdot AA_1}$ Let  $X'$  denote the Inverse of  $X$ By PoP we know   $$ AH \cdot AA_1=AC_1 \cdot AB=AB_1 \cdot AC $$   This means   $$ \odot(AH) \iff BC  $$     $$ H \iff A_1 $$     $$ \odot(ABC) \iff B_1C_1  $$   Now by using [Property 1 from the same post from the great Math Pi Rate's blog](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c701535h1754217) we get that the Queue point  $Q$  maps to the Ex point =  $G$ Now our task is to show that  $G,D',E'$  are collinear

Now notice that  $AD'=AE'$  because   $$ AF \cdot AF' = r^2 = AD \cdot AD' \implies AD' = AE' $$   and since  $D-H-E$  was a line  $AD'A_1E'$  would be cyclic
This is the end of the Inversion part  $\square$ Now we can restate the problem as follows <blockquote>Let  $G$  be the Ex point of  $\triangle ABC$  and let  $A_1$  be the  $A$  altitude and similarly let the  $B,C$  altitudes be  $B_1$  and  $C_1$  respectively. Then there exists a line that intersects  $AB$  and  $AC$  at  $D'$  and  $E'$  respectively such that  $AD'=AE'$  and  $AD'A_1E'$  be a cyclic quadrilateral </blockquote>
