Let  $\angle CAB=2\alpha$   $\angle ABC=2\beta$  and  $\angle ACB=2\gamma$ Let  $AA_1 \cap B_1C_1 = X$ It is obvious that there is such a line with  $AD'=AE'$ To see this just take a line through  $G$  such that it makes an angle  $\angle AD'E' = 90 - \alpha$  and it satisfies the above condition
However the tricky part is to prove that  $AD'A_1E'$  is a cyclic quad
By EGMO lemma  $9.11$  we get that   $$ -1=(G,X;C_1,B_1)\stackrel{A}{=}(G,AA_1 \cap D'E';D',E') $$   But combining this with EGMO lemma  $9.18$  we get   $$ \angle D'A_1A=\angle E'A_1A $$  This is the end of the projective part  $\square$ Now applying LoS on  $\triangle AD'A_1$  and  $\triangle AE'A_1$  and dividing the two ratios so obtained we get   $$ \frac{D'A_1}{E'A_1}=\frac{\cos(2\beta)}{\cos(2\gamma)} $$  Applying LoS on  $\triangle BD'A_1$  and  $\triangle CE'A_1$  and dividing the so obtained ratios we get   $$ \frac{D'A_1}{E'A_1}=\frac{\cos(2\beta) \cdot  c\sin(2\beta) \cdot \sin(90 - \angle E'AA_1 + 2\gamma)}{\cos(2\gamma \cdot b\sin(2\gamma) \cdot \sin(90-\angle D'AA_1 + 2\beta))} =\frac{\cos(2\beta) \cdot \cos(\angle E'AA_1 - 2\gamma)}{\cos(2\gamma  \cdot \cos(\angle D'AA_1 - 2\beta))}  $$  Equating these two equations we see that the troublesome terms magically cancel out and are finally left with   $$ \cos(2\beta-\angle D'AA_1)=\cos(2\gamma-\angle E'AA_1) $$   Now we bound the expressions inside  $\cos$    $$ 0 < \angle D'AA_1=\angle E'AA_1 < 90 \implies -90 < 2\beta - 90 < 2\beta-\angle D'AA_1 <2\beta \ \ \text{and} \ \ -90 < 2\gamma - 90 < 2\gamma-\angle E'AA_1 <2\gamma $$   And since  $\cos$  is an even function we only need to check the cases where   $$ 2\beta-\angle D'AA_1=2\gamma-\angle E'AA_1 $$   and   $$ 2\beta-\angle D'AA_1 = -(2\gamma-\angle E'AA_1) $$   The first case gives us  $2\beta=2\gamma$  which is absurd since  $AB \not = AC$  and the second case gives us   $$ \angle DA'A_1=\angle EA'A_1 = \beta+\gamma = 90 - \alpha = \angle AD'E'=\angle AE'D' $$   which implies  $AD'A_1E'$  is cyclic, so inverting the figure back we get the desired conclusion and this is the end of this problem  :D  $\blacksquare$</details>
