If  $a_0 \ge 0,$  then observe that
\begin{align*}
    \lfloor a_{i+1} \rfloor &\le a_{i+1} 
    &= \lfloor a_i \rfloor \{ a_i \} 
    &< \lfloor a_i \rfloor,
\end{align*}
for all nonnegative integers  $i,$  so  $\lfloor a_i \rfloor$  is a strictly decreasing integer monovariant. Therefore, there will exist some  $a_j$  such that  $\lfloor a_j \rfloor =0,$  after which it will result that   $$ 0=a_{j+1}=a_{j+2}=a_{j+3}=\ldots, $$   as desired. 

Now, observe that if   $$ a_i=-k-1+\frac{k+1}{k+2} $$   for some positive integer  $k,$  then it is not difficult to see that   $$ -k-1+\frac{k+1}{k+2}=a_i=a_{i+1}=a_{i+2}=\ldots, $$   as desired, so suppose that   $$ a_i \neq -k-1+\frac{k+1}{k+2}. $$   We prove the following lemma for the case when  $a_0$  is negative and cannot be expressed in the above form:**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** If  $a_i<0$  for some nonnegative integer  $i$  and  $a_i \neq -k-1+\tfrac{k+1}{k+2}$  for all positive integers  $k,$  then  $-1 \le a_N<0$  for sufficiently large  $N.$  
*Proof.* We proceed with induction on  $-\lfloor a_i \rfloor.$ The base case, when  $-\lfloor a_i \rfloor=1,$  is obvious. 

Now, assume that whenever  $-\lfloor a_i \rfloor=k$  for some positive integer  $k$  (and  $a_i \neq -k+\tfrac{k}{k+1}$ ), we will have  $-1 \le a_N<0$  for sufficiently large  $N.$  Consider the case when  $-\lfloor a_i \rfloor=k+1,$  i.e.   $$ -k-1 \le a_i<-k. $$   Observe that
\begin{align*}
    \lfloor a_{i+1} \rfloor &= \lfloor \lfloor a_i \rfloor \{a_i\}\rfloor 
    &\ge \lfloor \lfloor a_i \rfloor \rfloor 
    &= \lfloor a_i \rfloor,
\end{align*}
so it follows that  $\lfloor a_i \rfloor$  is nondecreasing. Therefore, it suffices to show that the sequence   $$ \left\{a_n-\left(-k-1+\frac{k+1}{k+2}\right)\right\}_{n \ge i} $$   is unbounded because it will result that for sufficiently large  $M,$  we will have   $$ -k \le a_M<-k+1, $$   after which the inductive hypothesis will finish. Indeed, if we let   $$ \left| a_i-\left(-k-1+\frac{k+1}{k+2}\right)\right|=\varepsilon $$   for some  $\varepsilon>0$  (we know such a  $\varepsilon$  exists because we stated  $a_i \neq -k-1+\tfrac{k+1}{k+2}$  for all positive integers  $k$  in the claim), we will have
\begin{align*}
    \left|a_{i+1}-\left(-k-1+\frac{k+1}{k+2}\right)\right| &= \left|(-k-1)\left(\frac{k+1}{k+2} \pm \varepsilon\right)-\left(-k-1+\frac{k+1}{k+2}\right)\right| 
    &= (k+1)\varepsilon,
\end{align*}
so we inductively get   $$ \left|a_{i+h}-\left(-k-1+\frac{k+1}{k+2}\right)\right| =(k+1)^h \varepsilon, $$  which is clearly unbounded since  $k$  is a positive integer and  $\varepsilon$  is nonzero, concluding the inductive step. 

Hence, by the Principle of Mathematical Induction, we have shown that if  $a_i<0$  for some nonnegative integer  $i$  and  $a_i \neq -k-1+\tfrac{k+1}{k+2}$  for all positive integers  $k,$  then  $-1 \le a_N<0$  for sufficiently large  $N.$   $\blacksquare$ Now, observe that   $$ a_{N+1}=-a_N-1, a_{N+2}=a_N, a_{N+3}=-a_N-1, \ldots, $$   as desired, concluding the proof.  $\square$

