It is straightforward to derive that  $\angle ABJ = \tfrac12 B + 90^{\circ} - A$  and  $\angle JBC = \tfrac 12 B + A - 90^{\circ}$  whence we obtain that
\[ \sin\angle ABJ = \cos \left( A-\tfrac12 B \right)
	\text{ and }
	\sin \angle JCB = -\cos \left( A+\tfrac12 B \right).
\]
The Law of Sines then directly gives us the ratio in which  $BJ$  divides  $\overline{AC}$ . Since  $\overline{CJ}$  is also an angle bisector we conclude, that
\[ J = \left(
	a\cos\left( A+\frac12 B \right) :
	b\cos \left( A+\frac12 B) \right) :
	-c\cos \left( A-\frac12 B \right)\right). \]
is a working triple of barycentric coordinates with respect to triangle  $ABC$ .

Dilate  $K$  and  $L$  at  $A$  with factor  $2$  to  $L' = \left( a-c : 0 : 2(s-a) \right)$  and  $K' = \left( a-b : 2(s-a) : 0 \right)$ .  Then it suffices to verify that  $K'$ ,  $L'$  and  $J$  are collinear; that is \[
	0 = \det \left[
	\begin{array}{ccc}
		a \cos \left( A + \frac 12 B \right) & b \cos \left( A + \frac 12 B \right) & -c \cos \left( A - \frac 12 B \right) 
		a-c & 0 & 2(s-a) 
		a-b & 2(s-a) & 0
	\end{array}
	\right]. 
\] which is equivalent upon expanding and factoring out an extra  $2(s-a)$  to \[ \left( c(a-c) \right) \cos \left( A - \frac 12 B \right)
	= \left( b(a-b)-2a(s-a) \right) \cos \left( A + \frac 12 B \right). \]
We can expand  $b(a-b)-2a(s-a) = ab-b^2-a(b+c-a) = a^2-ac-b^2$ .  Also, we can expand the trig.  Hence it's equivalent to \[ \left( ac-c^2 \right) \left[ \cos A \cos \frac 12 B + \sin A \sin \frac 12 B \right]
= \left( a^2-ac-b^2 \right) \left[  \cos A \cos \frac 12 B - \sin A \sin \frac 12 B \right] \] or, grouping terms,
\[ \left( b^2-c^2-a^2+2ac \right) \cos A \cos \frac 12 B
	= \left( c^2-a^2+b^2 \right) \sin A \sin \frac 12 B. \]
Put  $b^2-c^2-a^2+2ac = (-a+b+c)(a+b-c)$  and  $c^2-a^2+b^2 = 2bc\cos A$ .  Also,  $\tan \tfrac 12 B = \tfrac{r}{s-b}$  and  $\sin A = \tfrac{a}{2R}$ .  Thus this becomes \[ 4(s-a)(s-c) = 2bc \cdot \frac{a}{2R} \cdot \frac{r}{s-b} \iff s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c) = sr \cdot \frac{abc}{4R} \] as desired.

