 Here is a length based barycentric solution. We use standard barycentric coordinates with respect to  $\triangle ABC$ . Let  $M$  denote the midpoint of  $AJ$ , and  $E=BJ\cap AC$ . Note that
 \[DC = AC-AD = b - 2c\cos A = b-2c\frac{b^2+c^2-a^2}{2bc} = b-\frac{b^2+c^2-a^2}{b}=\frac{a^2-c^2}{b}.\]
 By the angle bisector theorem we have
 \[DE/EC = DB/BC = c/a,\]
 and since  $DE+DC=DC=(a+c)\cdot\frac{(a-c)}{b}$ , we must have  $EC=\frac{a(a-c)}{b}$ . Thus,  $AE=b-EC=\frac{b^2-a^2+ac}{b}$ . Therefore,
 \[E = (EC:0:EA) = (a(a-c):0:b^2-a^2+ac).\]
 Now since  $J$  lies on  $BE$ , it has the form  $J=(a(a-c):t:b^2-a^2+ac)$ , and since it lies on the  $\angle C$  angle bisector, we have that  $t=b(a-c)$ . Thus,
 \[J=(a(a-c):b(a-c):b^2-a^2+ac).\]
 Note that the sum of these coordinates is  $b(a+b-c)=2b(s-c)$ , so we also have
 \[J=\left(\frac{a(a-c)}{2b(s-c)},\frac{b(a-c)}{2b(s-c)},\frac{b^2-a^2+ac}{2b(s-c)}\right).\]
Therefore,
\[M=\frac{1}{2}(A+J) = (2b(s-c)+a(a-c):b(a-c):b^2-a^2+ac).\]
It is well known that  $K=(s-b:s-a:0)$  and  $L=(s-c:0:s-a)$ . It suffices to show that  $M,K,L$  collinear, or that
\[\begin{vmatrix}
s-b & s-a & 0 
s-c & 0 & s-a 
2b(s-c)+a(a-c) & b(a-c) & b^2-a^2+ac
\end{vmatrix}=0.\]
The determinant expands out to
\[-(s-b)(s-a)b(a-c)-(s-a)((b^2-a^2+ac)(s-c)-(s-a)(2b(s-c)+a(a-c))),\]
so it suffices to show
\[(s-b)b(a-c) = -(b^2-a^2+ac)(s-c) + (s-a)(2b(s-c)+a(a-c)),\]
or that
\[(a+c-b)b(a-c) = -(b^2-a^2+ac)(a+b-c) + (b+c-a)(b(a+b-c)+a(a-c)).\]
This is evident upon expanding both sides, so the proof is complete.

