<span style="color:#f00">**Lemma (Generalized CRT, 2 moduli):**</span> Let  $a,b,m,n$  be positive integers. Then there exists integer  $x$  such that
\begin{align*}
x &\equiv a\pmod m 
x &\equiv b\pmod n
\end{align*}
if and only if  $\gcd(m,n)\mid a-b$ .
-----
With this lemma in hand we induct on  $n$  with the trivial base case  $n=1$ . Let  $m_0$  be a positive integer to be picked later. We want to pick  $m$  such that
\begin{align*}
m &\equiv m_0\pmod{\varphi(n)} 
m &\equiv -2^{m_0}\pmod n.
\end{align*}
Clearly, we will be done if  $m$  exists. It exists if and only if  $\gcd(n,\varphi(n))\mid 2^{m_0}+m_0$ . Apply induction hypothesis and we are done.

We will prove the problem by induction on  $n$ . If the problem is true for  $i = {\kern 1pt} \overline {1,n - 1} $ , consider two positive integers a,b satisfying \[\left\{ \begin{gathered}
  {2^a} + a \vdots d = \gcd \left( {n,\varphi \left( n \right)} \right) \hfill 
  b \equiv \frac{{{2^a} + a}}{d}\,\,\,\,\left( {\bmod \,\,\frac{n}{d}} \right) \hfill  
\end{gathered}  \right.\]  Let  $t = a - b\left( {n - \varphi \left( n \right)} \right)$  then \[\left\{ \begin{gathered}
  a - t \vdots \left( {n - \varphi \left( n \right)} \right) \hfill 
  {2^a} + t \vdots n \hfill  
\end{gathered}  \right.\] Now, there exists a positive integer  $x$  satisfying \[\left\{ \begin{gathered}
  x \equiv a\,\,\left( {\bmod \,\,\varphi \left( n \right)} \right) \hfill 
  x \equiv t\,\,\left( {\bmod \,\,n} \right) \hfill  
\end{gathered}  \right.\]

Pick an integer  $m$  such that \begin{align*}
m &\equiv m_0\pmod{  \varphi(n)} 
m &\equiv -2^{m_0}\pmod {n}.
\end{align*}
In order to have solutions to the congruencies  $\gcd(\varphi(n),n)|2^{m_0}+m_0$  which can be established by induction.

Your quintessential prime powers problem.

We strong induct on the largest prime divisor  $p$  of  $n$ . Note that if  $n = 2^\ell$  is a power of two, just take  $m = 2^\ell$ . So let  $p \geq 3$  be the largest prime that divides  $n$ , and suppose  $\nu_p(n) = \ell \geq 1$ ; set  $n = p^\ell k$ .

By the inductive hypothesis, there is an  $m'$  such that  $k \mid 2^{m'} + m$ . For any nonnegative integer  $i$ , we then also have  $k \mid 2^{m'+k\varphi(k)i} + k\varphi(k) i + m'$ . (Note that when  $k$  is even, we must have  $m' \geq \nu_2(k)$  by definition, so  $2^{m'+k\varphi(k)i} \equiv 0 \equiv 2^{m'} \pmod {2^{\nu_2(k)}}$ . Since  $\varphi\left(k/2^{\nu_2(k)}\right) \mid \varphi(k)$  this implies the result.)

Let  $r = k\varphi(k)$  and note that  $p \nmid r$  by maximality of  $p$ . It suffices to find an  $i$  such that  $p^\ell \mid 2^{m'+ri} + m' + ri = f(i)$ . We will do this by induction on  $\ell$ .

*Base Case:* When  $\ell = 1$ , fix  $i$  modulo  $p-1$ , such that  $2^{m'+ri}$  is fixed modulo  $p$ . Then, by Chinese Remainder Theorem, we can find a residue  $i$  mod  $p$  such that, combined,  $p \mid f(i)$ .

*Inductive Step:* Observe that the system of congruences
\begin{align*}
i &\equiv a \pmod {p^{\ell}} 
i &\equiv b \pmod {p^{\ell} - p^{\ell - 1}}
\end{align*}
has a solution if and only if  $a \equiv b \pmod {p^{\ell - 1}}$  by Chinese Remainder Theorem. In particular, it suffices to find two integers  $a$  and  $b$  congruent modulo  $p^{\ell - 1}$  such that  $p^{\ell} \mid 2^{m'+br} + m'+ar$  and take  $i$  a solution to the above congruence.

To do this, use the inductive step to find a  $b$  such that  $p^{\ell - 1} \mid f(b)$ . Now since  $p \nmid r$ , there exists an  $0 \leq s < p$  such that  $p^\ell \mid f(b) + rsp^{\ell - 1}$ , and we can take  $a = b + sp^{\ell - 1}$ , as needed. This completes the induction.

