Firstly, since  $AK/KB=DL/LC,$  we know that lines  $AD, KL,$  and  $BC$  are concurrent at some point, call it  $R.$  

Now, observe that
\begin{align*}
    \angle{RCD} &= \angle{RBA} 
    &= \angle{CBA} 
    &= \angle{CQD},
\end{align*}
so it results that line  $BC$  is tangent to  $(CDQ).$  Similarly, we find that line  $BC$  is also tangent to  $(ABP).$  

Therefore, it results that the homothety  $\mathcal{H}$  centered at  $R$  taking  $(CDQ)$  to  $(ABP)$  takes  $C$  to  $B.$  

Also, if we let  $X \neq Q$  denote the second intersection of line  $RQ$  with  $(CDQ),$  we know that
\begin{align*}
    \angle{DXC} &= \angle{DCB} 
    &= \angle{APB},
\end{align*}
implying that  $\mathcal{H}$  takes  $X$  to  $P.$  Therefore, we know that   $$ \frac{RP}{RX}=\frac{RB}{RC} \implies RP \cdot RC = RB \cdot RX. $$   Also, applying Power of a Point on  $R$  with respect to  $(CDQ),$  we know that   $$ RX \cdot RQ=RC^2 \implies RX = \frac{RC^2}{RQ}. $$   Substituting this into the previous equation, we get   $$ RB \cdot RC = RP \cdot RQ, $$   implying that  $BCPQ$  is cyclic, as desired.  $\square$

