We first show  $\angle BEA_1 = 90^{\circ}$ . Note that  $E$  is the inverse of  $D$  with respect to  $(J)$ . Let  $B^\ast$  and  $A^\ast$  denote the inverses of  $B$  and  $A$ . Since  $\measuredangle BDA_1 = \measuredangle BB^\ast A_1 = 90^{\circ}$ , those four points are cyclic. Since  $JB^\ast \cdot JB = JE \cdot JD$ , point  $E$  lies on that circle too. Hence  $\measuredangle BEA_1 = 90^{\circ}$ . The proof that  $\angle B_1EA = 90^{\circ}$  is similar; note that  $\measuredangle ADB_1 = \measuredangle AEB_1 = 90^{\circ}$  and  $JA \cdot JA^\ast = JE \cdot JD$ .


