<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $\triangle ABC$  be inscribed in the unit circle, and let  $O_A, O_B, O_C$  denote the circumcenters of  $\triangle AB_1C_1, \triangle BC_1A_1, \triangle CA_1B_1$  respectively. Then
\begin{align*}
|a|=|b|=|c|=|a_2|=|b_2|=|c_2|&=1 
\overline{o_a} &= \frac{o_a}{aa_2} 
\overline{o_b} &= \frac{o_b}{bb_2} 
\overline{o_c} &= \frac{o_c}{cc_2}
\end{align*}
Then we have two equations for each of  $a_1, b_1, c_1$  which gives a system we can solve to find  $o_a, o_b, o_c$ :
\begin{align*}
a_1 &= c + o_b - \frac{co_b}{b_2} = b + o_c - \frac{bo_c}{c_2} 
b_1 &= a + o_c - \frac{ao_c}{c_2} = c + o_a - \frac{co_a}{a_2} 
c_1 &= b + o_a - \frac{bo_a}{a_2} = a + o_b - \frac{ao_b}{b_2}
\end{align*}
This is written in a more standard form as
\begin{align*}
c_2o_b(b_2-c) - b_2o_c(c_2-b) &= b_2c_2(b-c) 
a_2o_c(c_2-a) - c_2o_a(a_2-c) &= c_2a_2(c-a) 
b_2o_a(a_2-b) - a_2o_b(b_2-a) &= a_2b_2(a-b)
\end{align*}
Using Cramer's Rule (don't laugh),  $$ o_a = \frac{\begin{vmatrix}
b_2c_2(b-c) & c_2(b_2-c) & b_2(b-c_2) 
c_2a_2(c-a) & 0 & a_2(c_2-a) 
a_2b_2(a-b) & a_2(a-b_2) & 0
\end{vmatrix}}{\begin{vmatrix}
0 & c_2(b_2-c) & b_2(b-c_2) 
c_2(c-a_2) & 0 & a_2(c_2-a) 
b_2(a_2-b) & a_2(a-b_2) & 0
\end{vmatrix}} $$  The numerator is  $$ a_2^2b_2c_2\left[-(b-c)(c_2-a)(a-b_2) + (b_2-c)(c_2-a)(a-b) + (b-c_2)(c-a)(a-b_2)\right] $$    $$ = a_2^2b_2c_2(b_2-c_2)(c-a)(a-b) $$  and the denominator is  $a_2b_2c_2\Delta$ , where  $$ \Delta = bcc_2-bcb_2+caa_2-cac_2+abb_2-aba_2+b_2c_2c-b_2c_2b+c_2a_2a-c_2a_2c+a_2b_2b-a_2b_2a $$  Thus by symmetry, we have
\begin{align*}
o_a &= \frac{a_2(b_2-c_2)(c-a)(a-b)}{\Delta} 
o_b &= \frac{b_2(c_2-a_2)(a-b)(b-c)}{\Delta} 
o_c &= \frac{c_2(a_2-b_2)(b-c)(c-a)}{\Delta}
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
a_3 &= b+c-a_1 
b_3 &= c+a-b_1 
c_3 &= a+b-c_1
\end{align*}
So we find the vector
\begin{align*}
b_3-c_3 &= c-b+c_1-b_1 
&= \frac{co_a}{a_2}-\frac{bo_a}{a_2} 
&= -\frac{(b_2-c_2)(b-c)(c-a)(a-b)}{\Delta}
\end{align*}
Similarly,  $$ c_3-a_3 = -\frac{(c_2-a_2)(b-c)(c-a)(a-b)}{\Delta} $$  and so  $$ \frac{b_3-c_3}{a_3-c_3} = \frac{b_2-c_2}{a_2-c_2} $$  Thus the triangles are similar and oriented the same way.</details>

