The crux of the proof is the following bound: \begin{align*}
    \frac{1 + x^{2k}}{1 + x^{4k}} < \frac{1}{x^k}.
\end{align*} It's equivalent to prove \begin{align*}
    0 < x^4 - x^3 - x + 1 = (x - 1)^2(x^2 + x + 1),
\end{align*} which is true for  $x$  over the interval  $(0, 1)$ . Using the bound yields \begin{align*}
    \text{LHS} &< \left(\frac 1 x + \frac{1}{x^2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x^n} \right) \cdot \left(\frac 1 y + \frac{1}{y^2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{y^n} \right) 
    &= \frac{1}{x^n} \cdot \frac{1}{y^n} \cdot \frac{(1 - x^n)(1 - y^n)}{(1 - x)(1 - y)} 
    &= \frac{1}{(1 - x)(1 - y)},
\end{align*} as desired.

