We wish to show that  $2^{3k-1} || 2^{3k}(\mbox{some terms}) + (2^k - 1)!!(2^{2k} S_2 + 2^{2k-1} S_3)$ , i.e.,  $2^{k-1} || S_2 + \frac{S_1}{2}$ . This is clearly equivalent to  $S_2 + \frac{S_1}{2} \equiv 2^{k-1} \pmod{2^k}$ . Since  $\{\frac{1}{1}, \frac{1}{3}, \cdots, \frac{1}{2^k - 1}\}$  is a permutation of  $\{1, 3, \cdots, 2^k - 1\}$  modulo  $2^{k}$  and  $2^k - a \equiv -a \pmod{2^k}$ , we have: 
