<blockquote>Let  $n \geq 3$  be a positive integer. Find the maximum number of diagonals in a regular  $n$ -gon one can select, so that any two of them do not intersect in the interior or they are perpendicular to each other.</blockquote>
<details><summary>Hidden due to length</summary>We claim that for odd  $n,$  the answer is  $n-3,$  and for even  $n,$  the answer is  $n-2.$  The constructions are easy, draw all the diagonals from one vertex  $v.$  For odd  $n$  that's enough, and for even  $n,$  also join the vertices adjacent to the antipode of  $v.$  

<span style="color:#00f">**Lemma:**</span> Let the vertices be labelled  $0,1,\cdots n-1.$  Then  $(i,j) \perp (k,l) \Leftrightarrow |(i+j)-(k+l)|=\tfrac{n}{2}.$  In particular, for odd  $n,$  we can't have any perpendicular diagonals.
\end{tcolorbox}
<span style="color:#f00">*Proof:*</span> Consider the circle as the unit circle and the polygon's vertices as the  $n$ th roots of unity, labelling  $i$  by  $\zeta^i.$  Since  $AX \perp BC \Leftrightarrow ax+bc=0,$  hence if two diagonal  $(i,j),(k,l)$  are perpendicular, then this is equivalent to  $\zeta^{i+j}+\zeta^{k+l}=0.$  Then  $\zeta^{|(i+j)-(k+l)|}=-1.$  Squaring (and a bit of bounding) gives the result. Further, it can be checked that  $|(i+j)-(k+l)|=\tfrac{n}{2}$  gives  $\zeta^{i+j}+\zeta^{k+l}=0.$  Hence we have the lemma.  $\square$ ----------
So, for odd  $n$  it suffices to show that  $n-3$  is the maximum number of non-intersecting diagonals,  $d(n).$  This can be done inductively. The base cases are obvious. Let a diagonal  $d$  be drawn, and let the number of vertices by  $a,b$  on its right, left. Then, the number of non-intersecting diagonals is  $$ d(n) \le d(a+2)+d(b+2)+1 \le (a-1)+(b-1)+1=(a+b+2)-3=n-3 $$  Hence the induction is complete. 
(Also notice that this proof is independent of the parity of  $n,$  hence  $d(n) \le n-3$  for even  $n$  also)

Now, we claim the following:**<span style="color:#00f">Lemma:</span>** Consider any two pairs of perpendicular diagonals. The they must be "aligned" in the same way. More rigorously, if  $(d_1,d_2),(\ell_1,\ell_2)$  are two perpendicular diagonal pairs, then  $d_1 \parallel \ell_1$  or  $d_1 \parallel \ell_2.$ <span style="color:#f00">*Proof:*</span> Assume not, any let  $d_1,d_2$  divide the circle into  $4$  regions  $c_1,c_2,c_3,c_4.$  Then  $\ell_1,\ell_2$  must be contained completely within one of these regions, else it would intersect one of the diagonals  $d_1,d_2$  and hence must be perpendicular to that diagonal (and hence parallel to the other one). Now clearly no diameter is contained in any of these  $4$  regions, and so we have a contradiction.  $\square$ ------------
