This clearly extends naturally, and gives  $n-2$  diagonals, so have the required construction.

We now show that this is the maximum. If  $n$  is odd, then we make the following claim:

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim:**</span>In a regular  $n$ -gon, for odd  $n$ , there exists no two perpendicular diagonals.
[i]Proof.*Assume for the sake of contradiction that there does exist two perpendicular diagonals  $\overline{AB}$ , and  $\overline{CD}$ . Then, we know that \[m(AC)+m(BD) = 180^\circ\] so minor arcs  $AC$  and  $BD$  contain  $\tfrac{n-4}{2}$  of the remaining vertices, which implies that  $n$  is even, contradiction.  $\square$ Hence, the maximum for odd  $n$  is  $n-3$ . We now show the required bound if  $n$  is even. Let  $\mathcal{P}$  be the set of all diagonals that intersect another diagonal.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim:**</span>All diagonals in  $\mathcal{P}$  are pairwise parallel or perpendicular.
*Proof.*Let  $X, Y \in \mathcal{P}$  be two intersecting diagonals. Then, notice that for any other diagonal  $Z \in \mathcal{P}$ , either  $Z$ , or a diagonal that is intersecting with  $Z$  will intersection  $X$  or  $Y$ . Hence, all diagonals in  $\mathcal{P}$  are pairwise parallel or perpendicular, as required.  $\square$ Now, notice that the longest diagonal in each direction cannot form a rectangle with three other diagonals, since the side that comes out from the same vertex as the longest diagonal must have a diagonal perpendicular to it which is longer, contradicting maximality. Hence, the maximum number of vertices that aren't an endpoint of some element of  $\mathcal{P}$  is just  $n-|\mathcal{P}|-2$ . Now, notice that the maximum number of diagonals that can be drawn between these vertices is just  $n-|\mathcal{P}|-2$  as well. Hence, the maximum number of diagonals is \[n-|\mathcal{P}|-2+|\mathcal{P}| = n-2\] as required.  $\square$

This problem was proposed by me. :)**First Solution (Joshua Lee):** Letting  $D_1$  be the extouch point, we observe that \[ \measuredangle\left( \overline{XM}, \overline{AD_1} \right) = \measuredangle XMI = \measuredangle XBF = \measuredangle XBA \] since  $\overline{IM} \parallel \overline{AD_1}$ , and  $\triangle XBF \sim \triangle XMI$  by spiral similarity.

Thus  $\overline{XM}$  and  $\overline{AD_1}$  meet on  $\Gamma$ . Now by the *Butterfly Theorem*,  $\overline{XD}$  and  $\overline{AM}$  meet on  $\Gamma$  as well.**Second Solution (mine)**: Let  $\omega_A$  denote the  $A$ -mixtilinear incircle, tangent to  $\overline{AB}$  and  $\overline{AC}$  at  $F$  and  $E$ . Suppose  $\omega_A$  is tangent to  $\Gamma$  at  $T$ . Again let line  $AM$  meet  $\Gamma$  again at  $K$ . Let  $D_1$  and  $T_1$  be the reflections of  $D$  and  $T$  with respect to the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{BC}$ .



