[/asy]**Solution:** Note that  $E, F$  are none other than the touch points of the  $A$ -mixtilinear incircle on  $\overline{AC}$  and  $\overline{AB}$ . Let  $Z$  be the second intersection of  $\overline{AM}$  with  $\Gamma$ . We will prove that  $\overline{XD}$  passes through  $Z$ .

Consider a  $\sqrt{bc}$  inversion (an inversion around the circle centered at  $A$  with radius  $\sqrt{bc}$ , followed by a reflection about the  $A$ -angle bisector). Under this inversion,  $E, F$  are mapped to the touch points of the  $A$ -excircle on  $\overline{AB}$  and  $\overline{AC}$ , respectively. It follows that  $(AEF)$  is mapped to  $\overline{E^\ast F^\ast}$ . Also,  $(ABC)$  and  $\overline{BC}$  swap. Hence,  $X^\ast = \overline{E^\ast F^\ast} \cap \overline{BC}$ .

Now, we claim that  $ADZX^\ast$  is cyclic. By Power of a Point, it suffices to prove that
\begin{align*}
		MX^\ast \cdot MD &= MZ \cdot MA 
		&= MB^2 
		&= \left(\frac{a}{2}\right)^2. 
	\end{align*}Note that
\begin{align*}
		\angle BE^\ast X^\ast &= 90^\circ + \frac{A}{2} 
		\angle E^\ast X^\ast B &= \frac{C - B}{2} 
		BE^\ast &= CD
	\end{align*}(the first two equalities are just angle chasing, while the third is well known). By the Law of Sines,
\begin{align*}
		\frac{BE^\ast}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)} &= \frac{BX^\ast}{\sin\left(90^\circ + \frac{A}{2}\right)} 
		BX^\ast &= CD \cdot \frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)}.
	\end{align*}Then,
\begin{align*}
		MX^\ast &= \frac{a}{2} + CD \cdot \frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)} 
		MD &= \frac{a}{2} - CD.
	\end{align*}Now, we have
\begin{align*}
		MX^\ast \cdot MD &= \left(\frac{a}{2} + CD \cdot \frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)}\right)\left(\frac{a}{2} - CD\right) 
		&= \left(\frac{a}{2}\right)^2 + \frac{a}{2} \cdot CD \left(\frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)} - 1\right) - CD^2 \cdot \frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)}. 
	\end{align*}Noting that  $CD = \frac{a + b - c}{2}$ , we have
\begin{align*}
		MX^\ast \cdot MD &= \left(\frac{a}{2}\right)^2 
		\iff \left(\frac{a}{2}\right)^2 + \frac{a}{2} \cdot CD \left(\frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)} - 1\right) - CD^2 \cdot \frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)} &= \left(\frac{a}{2}\right)^2 
		\iff \frac{a}{2}\left(\frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)} - 1\right) - CD \cdot \frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)} &= 0 
		\iff \left(\frac{a}{2} - CD\right)\left(\frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)}\right) - \frac{a}{2} &= 0 
		\iff \left(\frac{c - b}{2}\right)\left(\frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)}\right) - \frac{a}{2} &= 0 
		\iff (c - b)\left(\frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)}\right) - a &= 0. 
	\end{align*}Finally, note by the Law of Sines that  $a = 2R\sin A$ , etc. Now,
\begin{align*}
		(c - b)\left(\frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)}\right) - a &= 0 
		\iff \left(\sin C - \sin B\right)\left(\frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)}\right) - \sin A &= 0 
		\iff \left(2\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)\cos\left(\frac{B + C}{2}\right)\right)\left(\frac{\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{C - B}{2}\right)}\right) - \sin A &= 0 
		\iff 2\cos\left(90^\circ - \frac{A}{2}\right)\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right) - \sin A &= 0 
		\iff 2\sin\left(\frac{A}{2}\right)\cos\left(\frac{A}{2}\right) - \sin A &= 0,
	\end{align*}which is just the double angle identity (the third equality is due to the sum to product formula). Thus,
\begin{align*}
		MX^\ast \cdot MD &= MZ \cdot MA,
	\end{align*}so  $ADZX^\ast$  is cyclic, as claimed.

To finish, we show that  $AX^\ast D^\ast Z^\ast$  is cyclic, which is enough to imply the desired collinearity of  $X, D, Z$ . We first show that  $X^\ast, Z, D^\ast$  are collinear. Indeed,
\begin{align*}
		\angle DZD^\ast &= \angle AZD^\ast - \angle AZD 
		&= \angle ADZ^\ast - \angle AZD 
		&= (180^\circ - \angle ADX^\ast) - \angle AX^\ast D 
		&= XA^\ast D 
		&= 180^\circ - \angle X^\ast ZD, 
	\end{align*}so  $X^\ast, Z, D^\ast$  are collinear. Finally,
\begin{align*}
		\angle D^\ast X^\ast Z^\ast &= \angle ZX^\ast D 
		&= \angle ZAD 
		&= \angle Z^\ast A D^\ast,
	\end{align*}so  $AX^\ast D^\ast Z^\ast$ . Inverting back, we find that  $\overline{XD}$  passes through  $Z$ . Thus,  $\overline{AM}$  and  $\overline{XD}$  meet on  $\Gamma$ , as desired.  $\Box$

