Now we show this is optimal. The main claim is the following.**Lemma**:  	In an arbitrary domino tiling of a  $2n \times 2n$  square 	it is possible to partition the dominoes into several cycles, 	at least  $n$  of which are nontrivial. 	(A cycle is a  $2k$ -cycle on the grid which pair off into dominoes, 	possibly including trivial cycles for which  $k = 1$ .)

*Proof.*  	Take the tiling and reflect it across the main diagonal, 	then overlay it on the original. 	This gives us a bunch of cycles. 	Now note every cell on the main diagonal is in a nontrivial cycle, 	and moreover by symmetry each such cycle has exactly two 	cells on the main diagonal.  $\blacksquare$ Thus, we can take a cycle with at most one marked square and then replace it with the overlaid image.

