Firstly we will show that  $X$  lies on the minor arc  $AC$  of  $(ACD)$ . Let  $X'$  be the humpty point of  $\triangle ABC$ , then  $X'$  lies on  $(ACD)$ , moreover,  $$ \angle BXD=\angle BXC+\angle XCD=180^{\circ}-\angle BCA+\angle CAD=\angle BPD $$  Hence  $X'$  lies on  $(BPD)$ , which implies  $X=X'$ .
Similarly,  $Y$  lies on the minor arc  $AC$  of  $(ABC)$ , now let  $P'$  be the projection of  $P$  on  $AC$ , then 
CLAIM.  $P'$  and  $Q$  either both lie inside  $\angle EPF$  or both lie outside  $\angle EPF$ Proof. 
Case I:  $E,F$  lies on the same side of  $M$ , then  $EP,EF$  lies on the same side of  $EX$ , similarly  $FP,FE$  lies on the same side of  $FY$ . Hence both  $P'$  and  $Q$  lie inside  $\angle EPF$ .
Case II:  $E,F$  lies on different side of  $M$ , then similarly  $P'$  and  $Q$  lie outside  $\angle EPF$ . $\blacksquare$ This eliminates the case issue, hence it suffices to show that   $$ \frac{\sin\angle EPQ}{\sin\angle FPQ}=\frac{\sin\angle EPP'}{\sin\angle FPP'} $$  Notice that the R.H.S. equals  $\displaystyle\frac{\cos\angle BEC}{\cos\angle DFC}$ .
Meanwhile, by Ceva's theorem, 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin\angle EPQ}{\sin\angle FPQ}&=\frac{\sin\angle BCX}{\sin\angle ECX}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle YCF}{\sin\angle YCD}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle XBE}{\sin\angle XBC}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle YDC}{\sin\angle YDF}
&=\frac{BX}{YD}\cdot\frac{CD}{BC}\cdot\frac{YM}{MX}\cdot\frac{ME}{BE}\cdot\frac{BC}{MC}\cdot\frac{MC}{CD}\cdot\frac{FD}{MF}
&=\frac{BX}{YD}\cdot\frac{MB}{MD}\cdot\frac{ME}{BE}\cdot\frac{FD}{MF}
&=\frac{ME}{MF}\frac{\sin\angle BEC}{\sin\angle DFC}
&=\frac{MM_1}{MM_2}\frac{\tan\angle DFC}{\tan\angle BEC}
&=\frac{\cos\angle BEC}{\cos\angle DFC}
\end{align*}
as desired.

