Now, plugging this back into  $P(x,x)$  we easily have that
\begin{align*}
    f(x)f(f(x)) + f(xf(x)) &= f(x^2)2f(f(x^2))
    f(x)f(f(x)) + \frac{f(f(x))}{x} &= \frac{f(x)}{x} \frac{2f(f(x))}{f(x)}
    f(x)+ \frac{1}{x} &= \frac{2}{x}
    f(x) &= \frac{1}{x}
\end{align*}
which was the required conclusion. Thus, we directly have that the only functions satisfying the original equation are in fact of the claimed type.

The only function that works is <details><summary>Click to expand</summary>$f(x)=\frac{1}{x}$</details>, which clearly works. Let's now prove this is the only one. $f(x)=xf(x^2)$  and  $f(f(x)^2)=f(f(x^2))$ <details><summary>proof</summary>\begin{align*}        
        P(1, 1) \implies & f(1)f(f(1))+f(f(1))=2f(1)f(f(1)) 
