Note  $AC$  is tangent to  $(APR)$  since  $\angle QAC = \angle QDC = \angle QPA.$  It follows 
\begin{align*}
\angle ABC &= \angle BAC 
&= \angle BAR + \angle CAR 
&= \angle BAR + \angle APR 
&= 180^\circ - \angle ARP 
&= \angle ARC,
\end{align*}
so quadrilateral  $ABRC$  is cyclic. Define  $K = BR \cap (APR).$  Further angle chasing gives
\begin{align*}
\angle KAP &= \angle KRP 
&= \angle BAC 
&= \angle ABC 
&= \angle ADC,
\end{align*}
and because  $AB \parallel CD$  it follows  $K,A,D$  are collinear. Then it folows
\begin{align*}
\angle KRP &= \angle BAC 
&=\angle ABC 
&= \angle ADC 
&= \angle KDC,
\end{align*}
so  $KRCD$  is cyclic. Hence by radical axes on  $(ARP), (AQC), (KRCD),$  the lines concur.

