The case  $n=2$  is trivial, suppose  $n \ge 3$ . First we homogenize the inequality, it remains to prove that  $$ \sum_{k=2}^{n} \frac{a_k}{a_1+a_2+\dots +a_n -a_k} (a_1+a_2+\dots+a_{k-1})^2 < \frac{(a_1+a_2+\dots+a_n)^2}{3} $$  let  $S$  denote the quantity on the LHS. We have the identity

\begin{align*}
S &=\sum_{k=2}^{n} a_k(a_1+a_2+\dots+a_{k-1})-\frac{a_k(a_1+a_2+\dots+a_{k-1})(a_{k+1}+a_{k+2}+\dots+a_n) }{a_1+a_2+\dots+a_n-a_k}
&= \sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} a_i a_j - \sum_{k=2}^{n} \frac{a_k(a_1+a_2+\dots+a_{k-1})(a_{k+1}+a_{k+2}+\dots+a_n) }{a_1+a_2+\dots+a_n-a_k}
&< \sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} a_i a_j -\frac{\displaystyle{\sum_{k=2}^{n} a_k(a_1+a_2+\dots+a_{k-1})(a_{k+1}+a_{k+2}+\dots+a_n)}}{a_1+a_2+\dots +a_n}
&= \sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} a_i a_j - \frac{\displaystyle{\sum_{1 \le i <j<k \le n} a_i a_j a_k}}{a_1+a_2+\dots+a_n}
\end{align*}
thus it suffices to prove that  $$ (a_1+a_2+\dots+a_n)^3 > 3(a_1+a_2+\dots+a_n)\sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} a_i a_j -  3\sum_{1 \le i <j<k \le n} a_i a_j a_k $$  indeed, notice that
\begin{align*}
3(a_1+a_2+\dots+a_n)\sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} a_i a_j -  3\sum_{1 \le i <j<k \le n} a_i a_j a_k
&= 3\sum_{1 \le i \neq j \le n} a_i^2 a_j + 9 \sum_{1 \le i <j<k \le n} a_i a_j a_k - 3\sum_{1 \le i <j<k \le n} a_i a_j a_k 
&= 3\sum_{1 \le i \neq j \le n} a_i^2 a_j + 6 \sum_{1 \le i <j<k \le n} a_i a_j a_k
\end{align*}
while  $$ (a_1+a_2+\dots+a_n)^3 =\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i^3 +3\sum_{1 \le i \neq j \le n} a_i^2 a_j + 6 \sum_{1 \le i <j<k \le n} a_i a_j a_k $$  as desired.

Assume for contradiction not. Fix any  $u \in S$ .  	
- By pigeonhole, take a divisor  $d \mid u$  	such that  $D \coloneqq \{s \in S : \gcd(s,u) = d\}$  is infinite.
- Because of our contradiction assumption, 	any two elements of  $D$  also have gcd  $d$ .
- Since the gcd's are not all the same, 	there is some  $v$  with  $d' \coloneqq \gcd(u,v) \neq d$ . 	(If there wasn't, every pair of elements would now have gcd  $d$ .)
- Since  $|D| = \infty$ , 	we can find a divisor  $e \mid v$  such that 	two elements  $w_1, w_2 \in D$  have  $\gcd(w_1, v) = \gcd(w_2, v) = e$ .
- If  $e \neq d$ , take  $(x,y,z) = (w_1, v, w_2)$ . 	If  $e = d$ , take  $(x,y,z) = (u,w_1,v)$ .

