Suppose that the equation is true. Then note that
\[\sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n \left\{\frac{ij}{n+1}\right\}=\sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n \left(\frac{ij}{n+1}-\left\lfloor \frac{ij}{n+1} \right\rfloor\right)=\frac{n^2(n+1)}{4}-\frac{n^2(n-1)}{4}=\frac{n^2}{2}.\]
It is important to note that  $\left\{\frac{ij}{n+1}\right\}=\frac{r}{n+1}$  where  $0\le r\le n$  is the remainder when  $ij$  is divided by  $n+1$ . Let  $r_{ij}$  denote this value for some fixed  $i,j$ . Fix  $i$  and suppose that  $\gcd{(i, n+1)}=k$  where  $i=kx$  and  $n+1=ky$ . Then we have that 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{j=1}^n \left\{\frac{ij}{n+1}\right\}&=\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{j=1}^{n+1}r_{ij}
&=\frac{k}{n+1}\sum_{j=1}^{y}r_{ij}
&=\frac{n+1-k}{2}
\end{align*}
which implies that
\[\sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n \left\{\frac{ij}{n+1}\right\}\le \frac{n^2}{2}\]
with equality when all numbers from  $1$  to  $n$  are relatively prime to  $n+1$ . Then  $n+1$  is prime so we are done.  $\blacksquare$

Note that
