\sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n \left\lfloor \frac{ij}{n+1} \right\rfloor &= \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n \frac{ij}{n+1} - \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n \left\{ \frac{ij}{n+1} \right\}
&= \frac{n^2(n+1)}{4} - \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n \left\{ \frac{ij}{n+1} \right\},
\end{align*}
so we need  $\sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n \left\{ \frac{ij}{n+1} \right\} = \frac{n^2}{2}$ . We will now use to fact that  $\left\{\frac{i(n + 1 - j)}{n+1}\right\} = \left\{ - \frac{ij}{n+1}\right\}$ , which equals  $1 - \left\{ \frac{ij}{n+1}\right\}$  when  $ij$  is not divisible by  $n+1$  and is  $0$  otherwise. This implies that   $$ \left\{\frac{ij}{n+1}\right\} + \left\{\frac{i(n+1 - j)}{n+1}\right\} = \begin{cases}1 & (n+1)\nmid ij  0 & (n+1)|ij\end{cases}. $$  We now take cases on  $n\mod 4$ .

First, if  $n\equiv 0,2\pmod{4}$ , then if we pair terms  $(i,j)$  and  $(i, n+1-j)$  in the sum, there will be  $n^2/2$  such pairs, each with a sum at most  $1$ , meaning the entire sum is at most  $n^2/2$ . Then, equality must hold everywhere. That is, each pair must have a sum of exactly one, meaning  $n+1\nmid ij$  for all  $1\le i,j\le n$ . Clearly this occurs if and only if  $n + 1$  is an odd prime.

If  $2\nmid n$ , then note that  $n\equiv 1\pmod{4}$  since  $\frac{n^2(n-1)}{4}\in\mathbb{Z}$  (from the original problem statement). Then, there will be exactly  $\frac{n^2 - n}{2}$  pairs, which sum to at most  $\frac{n^2 - n}{2}$  in total. There will also be  $n$  unpaired terms of the form  $\left\{\frac{i\cdot \frac{n+1}{2}}{n+1}\right\} = \left\{\frac{i}{2}\right\}$ , which, in total, sum to  $\frac{n+1}{4}$ . Hence, the entire sum is at most  $\frac{n^2 - n}{2} + \frac{n+1}{4}$ , which is clearly less than  $n^2/2$  for  $n > 1$ . If  $n$  is exactly  $1$ , we can easily check that the condition is satisfied. Hence,  $n =1$  is the only solution here.

Thus the only solutions are all  $n$  for which  $n + 1$  is prime.

