The answer is  $\boxed{\{n\}}$  and  $\boxed{\{n,\,3n\}}$  for all positive integers  $n$ . Clearly these work.

Divide  $\mathcal{S}$  by the gcd so WLOG assume  $\gcd(\mathcal{S})=1$ . Assume  $\mathcal{S}$  has more than one element. Then  $\mathcal{S}$  contains an odd number. If  $\mathcal{S}$  had an even number, then letting  $a$  be even and  $b$  be odd gives a contradiction. Thus every element of  $\mathcal{S}$  is odd.

Let  $m:=\max(\mathcal{S})$ . For all  $n\in\mathcal{S}\setminus\{m\}$ , there exists  $c\in\mathcal{S}$  such that  $m\mid n+2c$ . Since  $n+2c<3m$ , either  $m=n+2c$  or  $2m=n+2c$ . The latter is impossible so  $c=\frac{m-n}{2}\in\mathcal{S}\setminus\{m\}$ . Fix  $n\in\mathcal{S}\setminus\{m\}$ . We wish to prove that  $m=3n$ .**Claim.**  $\frac{\frac{2\cdot 4^k+1}{3}\cdot m-n}{2\cdot 4^k}\in\mathcal{S}$  for all integers  $k\geq 0$ .
*Proof.* Induction on  $k$ . For the induction step,
\begin{align*}
\frac{\frac{2\cdot 4^k+1}{3}\cdot m-n}{2\cdot 4^k}&\in\mathcal{S}
\implies\frac{\frac{4^{k+1}-1}{3}\cdot m+n}{4^{k+1}}&\in\mathcal{S}
\implies\frac{\frac{2\cdot 4^{k+1}+1}{3}\cdot m-n}{2\cdot 4^{k+1}}&\in\mathcal{S}.\ \square
\end{align*}

If  $3n>m$  or  $3n<m$ , then  $\frac{\frac{2\cdot 4^k+1}{3}\cdot m-n}{2\cdot 4^k}<\frac{\frac{2\cdot 4^{k+1}+1}{3}\cdot m-n}{2\cdot 4^{k+1}}$  or  $\frac{\frac{2\cdot 4^k+1}{3}\cdot m-n}{2\cdot 4^k}>\frac{\frac{2\cdot 4^{k+1}+1}{3}\cdot m-n}{2\cdot 4^{k+1}}$  for all  $k\geq 0$ , respectively, contradicting the finiteness of  $\mathcal{S}$ . Thus  $3n=m$ .  $\square$

