Note that we always have  $\frac{a_{n-1}+a_{n+1}}{2}>\sqrt{a_{n-1}a_{n+1}}$ . Define \[\Delta ^{\div}a_n=\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\] Note
\begin{align*}
    &a_n=\frac{a_{n-1}+a_{n+1}}{2}\implies \frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n}+\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=2\implies \Delta^\div a_n=2-\frac{1}{\Delta^\div a_{n-1}}
    &a_n=\sqrt{a_{n-1}a_{n+1}}\implies a_{n-1}(a_{n+1}-a_n)=a_n(a_n-a_{n-1})\implies \Delta a_n=(\Delta^\div a_{n-1})(\Delta a_{n-1})
\end{align*}
Then for all  $n\geq 1$ , we have
\begin{align*}
    &a_n=\frac{a_{n-1}+a_{n+1}}{2} && \vee && &&a_n=\sqrt{a_{n-1}a_{n+1}}
    \implies&\begin{cases} \Delta a_n=\Delta a_{n-1} \Delta^\div a_n=2-\frac{1}{\Delta^\div a_{n-1}}\end{cases} &&&& &\implies&\begin{cases}\Delta^\div a_n=\Delta^\div a_{n-1} \Delta a_n=(\Delta^\div a_{n-1})(\Delta a_{n-1})\end{cases}
\end{align*}
Now write  $\Delta^\div a_0=\frac{a_1}{a_0}$  as a fraction  $\frac{s_1}{s_0}$  in simplest form, and extend  $s_0,s_1$  into an increasing arithmetic sequence  $s_0,s_1,s_2,\dots$ . We now see \[2-\frac{1}{\frac{s_k}{s_{k-1}}}=\frac{2s_k-s_{k-1}}{s_k}=\frac{s_{k+1}}{s_k}\] We now have for all  $n\geq 1$ ,


- $b_n=A$  corresponds to keeping  $\Delta a_n$  the same as  $\Delta a_{n-1}$  and replacing  $\Delta^\div a_{n-1}=\frac{s_k}{s_{k-1}}$  with  $\Delta^\div a_n=\frac{s_{k+1}}{s_k}$ , and
- $b_n=G$  corresponds to keeping  $\Delta^\div a_n$  the same as  $\Delta^\div a_{n-1}$  and multiplying  $\Delta a_{n-1}$  by  $\Delta^\div a_{n-1}$  to get  $\Delta^\div a_n=(\Delta^\div a_{n-1})(\Delta a_{n-1})$ .


If  $\{b_n\}$  is eventually always  $G$ , then we are done, and so assume this is not the case. Now for every index  $n$  with  $b_n=A$ , we write  $\Delta^\div a_n=\frac{s_{k+1}}{s_k}$ , and consider the polynomial  $P$  with nonnegative integer coefficients formed by expressing  $\Delta a_n$  in base  $s_k$  and then replacing  $s_k$  with the indeterminate  $x$ . For some such polynomials  $S,T$ , we say  $S\prec T$  if  $T-S$  has positive leading coefficient. For example, we have
\begin{align*}
1230_{10}\to x^3+2x^2+3x
13_2\to x^3+x^2+1
x^3+x^2+1\prec x^3+2x^2+3x
\end{align*}
Then  $\preceq$  is a total ordering of polynomials with nonnegative integer coefficients.**Claim: The value of  $P$  is nonincreasing**
We will compare  $P$  before and after consecutive appearances of  $A$  in  $\{b_n\}$ . Suppose \[A\underbrace{GG\dots G}_tA\] appears in  $\{b_n\}$ . Let  $\Delta a_n$  and  $\Delta^\div a_n=\frac{s_{k+1}}{s_k}$  be the terms before the run of  $G$ 's. Since  $\gcd(s_k,s_{k+1})=1$  and  $\Delta a_{n+t+1}$  is an integer, we must have  $s_k^t\mid \Delta a_n$ . Now we have
\begin{align*}
\Delta a_{n+t+1}&=\left(\frac{s_{k+1}}{s_k}\right)^t(\Delta a_n)
\Delta^\div a_{n+t+1}&=\frac{s_{k+2}}{s_{k+1}}
\end{align*}
after the run of  $G$ 's. But we have that the polynomial produced by  $\frac{\Delta a_n}{s_k^t}$  in base  $s_{k+1}$  is at most the polynomial produced by  $\frac{\Delta a_n}{s_k^t}$  in base  $s_k$ . Multiplying these polynomials by  $x^t$ , it follows that the polynomial produced by  $\Delta a_{n+t+1}$  in base  $s_{k+1}$  is at most the polynomial produced by  $\Delta a_n$  in base  $s_k$ . Then  $P$  is nonincreasing.**Claim: The value of  $P$  is eventually constant**
Since  $\prec$  has no infinitely long descending chains, this follows from the above claim.**Claim: The eventually constant value of  $P$  is of the form  $cx^t$  for some  $c\geq 1,t\geq 0$**
Consider the eventually constant value of  $P$ . Consider consecutive appearances of  $A$  in  $\{b_n\}$  after such  $P$  is achieved. Take some such appearance \[A\underbrace{GG\dots G}_tA\] in  $\{b_n\}$ . Let  $\Delta a_n$  and  $\Delta^\div a_n=\frac{s_{k+1}}{s_k}$  be the terms before the run of  $G$ 's. Following the proof that the value of  $P$  is nonincreasing, we have that the polynomial produced by  $\left(\frac{s_{k+1}}{s_k}\right)^t(\Delta a_n)$  in base  $s_{k+1}$  is equal to the polynomial produced by  $\Delta a_n$  in base  $s_k$ . Dividing these polynomials by  $x^t$ , it follows that the the polynomials produced by  $\frac{\Delta a_n}{s_k^t}$  in base  $s_k$  and base  $s_{k+1}$  are the same. Since  $s_{k+1}>s_k$ , this is only possible if  $\frac{\Delta a_n}{s_k^t}$  is a single digit  $c$ . But then  $P=cx^t$ , as desired.**Claim: The distance between consecutive appearances of  $A$  in  $\{b_n\}$  is eventually always  $t$ .**
This follows immediately from the proof of the above claim.

The above claim implies that  $\{b_n\}$  is eventually periodic, as desired.

We claim the only such sequences are \[c,c,c,c,\dots\] for  $c\in(-\infty,\infty)$  and \[c,-c,c,-c,\dots\] for  $c\in\left[-\frac12,\frac12\right]$ . It is easy to check that these both work.

We now show that these are the only solutions. Consider a solution  $\{a_n\}$ . Let  $p$  be its period. We have that \[a_{n+2}+a_n^2=a_n+a_{n+1}^2\implies a_{n+2}-a_{n+1}=(a_{n+1}^2-a_{n+1})-(a_n^2-a_n)\implies \Delta a_{n+1}=(\Delta a_n)(a_{n+1}+a_n-1)\] We also have \[\Delta a_{n+1}=(\Delta a_n)(a_{n+1}+a_n-1)\implies a_{n+2}+a_{n+1}=a_{n+1}+a_n+\Delta a_{n+1}+\Delta a_n=(\Delta a_n+1)(a_{n+1}+a_n)\] Now we see  $\Delta a_n=0\implies \Delta a_{n+1}=0$ , and so if  $\Delta a_n=0$  then  $\{a_n\}$  is constant. Also,  $a_{n+1}+a_n=0\implies a_{n+2}+a_{n+1}=0$ , and so if  $a_{n+1}+a_n=0$  then  $\{a_n\}$  is of the form  $c,-c,c,-c,\dots$ , in which case we have  $c\in\left[-\frac12,\frac12\right]$ . Now assume  $\Delta a_n\neq 0$  and  $a_{n+1}+a_n\neq 0$  for all  $n$ . Now we have \[\prod_{n=1}^p(\Delta a_n+1)=\prod_{n=1}^p\left(\frac{a_{n+2}+a_{n+1}}{a_{n+1}+a_n}\right)=1\] We also already have \[\sum_{n=1}^p\Delta a_n=0\] Now partition the indices  $1,2,\dots,p$  into  $S$  and  $T$  so that  $n\in S$  if  $\Delta a_n>0$  and  $n\in T$  if  $\Delta a_n<0$ . Then  $S$  and  $T$  must both be nonempty. Let \[r=\sum_{n\in S}\Delta a_n=\sum_{n\in T}(-\Delta a_n)\] Then since  $-1<\Delta a_n\leq 1$  for all  $n$ , we have  $0<r<|T|$ . We now have
\begin{align*}
&\prod_{n=1}^p(\Delta a_n+1)=1
\implies&\left(\prod_{n\in S}(\Delta a_n+1)\right)\left(\prod_{n\in T}(\Delta a_n+1)\right)=1
\implies&\left(1+\frac{r}{|S|}\right)^{|S|}\left(1-\frac{r}{|T|}\right)^{|T|}\geq 1
\implies&\left(1+\frac{r}{|S|}\right)^{|S|}\geq \left(1+\frac{r}{|T|}+\left(\frac{r}{|T|}\right)^2+\cdots\right)^{|T|}
\implies&\left(1+\frac{r}{|S|}\right)^{|S|}>\left(1+\frac{r}{|T|}\right)^{|T|}
\implies&\left(1+\frac{1}{|S|/r}\right)^{|S|/r}>\left(1+\frac{1}{|T|/r}\right)^{|T|/r}
\implies&|S|>|T|
\end{align*}
The third line is by AM-GM Inequality and the last line is because  $\left(1+\frac{1}{x}\right)^x$  is increasing for  $x>0$ ; indeed, we have \[\frac{d}{dx}\left(1+\frac{1}{x}\right)^x=\left(1+\frac{1}{x}\right)^x\left(\ln\left(1+\frac{1}{x}\right)-\frac{1}{1+x}\right)=\left(1+\frac{1}{x}\right)^x\left(\int_{1}^{1+\frac{1}{x}}\frac{t-1}{t^2}\; dt\right)>0\] for  $x>0$ . Now from  $|S|>|T|$  it follows that  $\Delta a_{k+1},\Delta a_k>0$  for some  $k$ . However, we have
\begin{align*}
&\Delta a_{k+1},\Delta a_k>0
\implies& a_{k+1}+a_k=\frac{\Delta a_{k+1}}{\Delta a_k}+1>1\quad\wedge\quad \Delta a_{k+1},\Delta a_k>0
\implies& a_{k+2}+a_{k+1}=a_{k+1}+a_k+\Delta a_{k+1}+\Delta a_k>1\quad\wedge\quad \Delta a_{k+1},\Delta a_k>0
\implies& \Delta a_{k+2}=(\Delta a_{k+1})(a_{k+2}+a_{k+1}-1)>0
\end{align*}
Now repeating this argument, we have  $\Delta a_n>0$  for all  $n$ . However, this is impossible. Then there are no other solutions, as desired.

