for all  $n$ . We assign  $x_n$  to be the numerator, and  $y_n$  to be the denominator. Let  $\ell$  be the value such that  $(\varepsilon+\chi \varepsilon) \cdot 3^{\ell}  = 4^{\ell}$ , where  $\chi$  denotes the maximum value of  $f \coloneq \varepsilon3^x - 4^x.$  We prove the following main claim. **<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**  $\varepsilon x_n- y_n^2  \le \chi n$  and  $n \le \varepsilon 3^k$  where  $k \ge \operatorname{max}(a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n)$ *Proof.* Note, \begin{align*} \varepsilon(3^{a_1} + 3^{a_2} + \cdots + 3^{a_{n}})-(2^{a_1}+2^{a_2} + \cdots + 2^{a_{n}})^2   \le \sum_{i} \left( \varepsilon 3^{a_i} - 4^{a_i} \right ) = \sum_{i} f(a_i) \le \chi n \end{align*}
For the second part, note that  $\varepsilon x_n - y_n^2 \le n(\varepsilon 3^k - n)$ , by simply trivially bounding the numerator and denominator, so clearly  $n \le \varepsilon3^k$  as desired.  $\square$ **<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>** If  $(a_n)$  satisfies the above assumption, then  $a_n \le \ell$  for all  $n$ . 

