Let  $AK$  meet the circle  $(ABC)$  at point  $D$ . We have  $$ \overline{KZ} \cdot \overline{KY}=\overline{KB}\cdot \overline{KC}=\overline{KA}\cdot \overline{KD}, $$  therefore the points  $A$ ,  $D$ ,  $Y$ ,  $Z$  are concyclic.
Let  $AI$  meet  $ZY$  at  $E$ ,  $BC$  at  $G$ , and circle  $(ADYZ)$  at  $N \neq A$ .
Note that  $$ \frac{\overline{KD}}{\overline{KA}}=\frac{\overline{KD} \cdot \overline{KA}}{KA^2}=\frac{\mathbf{Power}(K, \odot (ABC))}{KA^2} $$    $$ \frac{\overline{EN}}{\overline{EA}}=\frac{\overline{EN}\cdot \overline{EA}}{EA^2}=\frac{\mathbf{Power}(E, \odot (BIC))}{EA^2} $$  Now, let  $A=(1,0,0)$ ,  $B=(0,1,0)$ ,  $C=(0,0,1)$  and  $$ K=\left(0,\frac{q}{q+r},\frac{r}{q+r}\right) $$  The coordinates of  $G$  are  $$ G=\left(0,\frac{b}{b+c},\frac{c}{b+c}\right) $$  Using the distance formula gives us  $$ KA^2=\frac{(b^2+c^2-a^2)qr+b^2r^2+c^2q^2}{(q+r)^2} $$     $$ KG=\frac{a(br-cq)}{(q+r)(b+c)} $$  Since  $KE$  is the internal angle bisector of  $\triangle AKG$ , we have  $$ E=\left(a(br-cq):bt:ct\right), \ \text{with }t=\sqrt{(b^2+c^2-a^2)qr+b^2r^2+c^2q^2} $$  Using the distance formula, we have  $$ EA^2=\frac{t^2bc(b+c-a)(b+c+a)}{(abr-acq+bt+ct)^2} $$  The equation of circle  $(ABC)$  is  $$ a^2yz+b^2zx+c^2xy=0 $$  The equation of circle  $(BIC)$  is  $$ a^2yz+b^2zx+c^2xy-bcx(x+y+z)=0 $$  From here, we can calculate  $$ \mathbf{Power}(K, \odot (ABC))=-(a^2y_Kz_K+b^2z_Kx_K+c^2x_Ky_K)=\frac{-a^2qr}{(q+r)^2} $$  \begin{align*}
    \mathbf{Power}(E, \odot (BIC))&=-(a^2y_Ez_E+b^2z_Ex_E+c^2x_Ey_E-bcx_E(x_E+y_E+z_E))
    &=\frac{a^2bc((br-qc)^2-t^2)}{(abr-acq+bt+ct)^2}=\frac{a^2bc((br-qc)^2-(b^2+c^2-a^2)qr-b^2r^2-c^2q^2)}{(abr-acq+bt+ct)^2}
    &=\frac{-a^2bcqr(b+c-a)(b+c+a)}{(abr-acq+bt+ct)^2}
\end{align*}
This leads to the conclusion that  $$ \frac{\overline{KD}}{\overline{KA}}=\frac{\overline{EN}}{\overline{EA}}=\frac{-a^2qr}{t^2}=\frac{-a^2qr}{(b^2+c^2-a^2)qr+b^2r^2+c^2q^2} $$  Using Thales' theorem, we have  $DN \parallel KE$ , or  $DN \parallel ZY$ , thus  $$ \angle EAY=\angle NAY=\angle ZAD $$  Using similar arguments, we also have  $\angle EAW=\angle XAD$ , which implies that  $$ \angle WAY=\angle EAW+\angle EAY=\angle XAD+\angle ZAD=\angle ZAX. $$

