In this triangle  $ABC$ , call  $M,N$  the midpoints,  $Y,Z$  the intersections of the perpendicular bisectors of  $AB,AC$  with  $AC,AB$  respectively. Then Pappus on  $BZN, CYM$  gives circumcenter, centroid and  $K=BY \cap CZ$  collinear, but  $K$  is the point for which  $\angle CAB=\angle ABY$  and  $\angle CAB= \angle ZCA$ , which is what we wanted.</details> to know that  $A$  lies on the Euler line of  $\triangle IKL$ . Since  $G \in AD$ , we have that  $AD$  is the Euler line of  $\triangle IKL$ .  $\blacksquare$

Define  $N$  as the antipode of  $B$  wrt  $\omega$ . We will show:


- $N \ne A$ .
- $N \in \odot(AKL)$
- $NA = NL$ .

This will show  $M \equiv N$  and  $MA = ML$ . Its clear  $N \ne A$ , otherwise  $C \equiv A$ , contradiction. Now  $AB = CD$  gives  $BC \parallel AD$ . As  $BC \perp CN$ , thus   $$  CN \perp AD  $$  Now note points  $K,B,N$  are collinear on the perpendicular bisector of segment  $AC$ . This also means   $$  KN \text{ bisects } \angle AKC \qquad \qquad (1)  $$  [asy]
