*Proof.* To prove  $(BC)$  is orthogonal to  $\Omega_O$ , it suffices to prove  $C$  lies on the polar of  $B$ , that is  $O_{AB} O_{BP}$ . We claim in fact that  $C = O_{AB} O_{BP} \cap O_{AQ} O_{QP}$ . By degenerate Brianchon on  $ABPQ$ , lines  $AP$ ,  $BQ$ ,  $O_{AB} O_{PQ}$ ,  $O_{AQ} O_{BP}$  concur at some point, which we name  $C^{\prime}$ . Additionally if  $Z=O_{AB} O_{BP} \cap O_{AQ} O_{QP}$ , then  $Z$  lies on  $AP$  by Pascal on  $O_{BP} O_{BP} O_{AB} O_{QP} O_{QP} O_{AQ}$ ; further,  $(C^{\prime}, Z; P, A) = O_{AB} (O_{PQ}, O_{BP}; P, O_{AB}) =  -1$  since line  $O_{AB} P$  is a symmedian. Finally, we have  $(C^{\prime}, Z; P, A) = -1=(Q,R;P, P_{\infty QR}) \stackrel B= (C^{\prime}, C; P, A) $  (the penultimate equality uses the fact  $P$  is the midpoint of  $QR$ , which follows from Ceva), thus giving our desired result. Similar reasoning applies to prove  $(BC)$  is orthogonal to  $\Omega_U$ .  $\blacksquare$ **Claim.** We have  $BO_{AB} = CU_{DC}$ .
*Proof.*  Note that  $\Delta DCO_{BP}$  is isosceles as  $\measuredangle DCO_{BP} = \measuredangle BO_{AB}C  = \measuredangle O_{AB} O_{BP} B = \measuredangle CO_{BP}D$ .  Hence  $\frac{AD+DB-AB}{2} = DO_{BP} = DC$  and similarly we obtain  $\frac{DA+AC-DC}{2} = AU_{CP} = AB$ ; subtracting these two equations implies  $DC-AB=BD-AC$ . However this implies   $$ 2 BO_{AB} + AD = 2 BO_{AB} + AO_{AB} + DO_{BP} = AB + BD = AC + CD = 2CU_{CD} + DU_{CD} + AU_{CP} = 2 CU_{CD} + AD $$   and hence  $BO_{AB} = CU_{DC}$  as desired.   $\blacksquare$ We now say two points  $K$ ,  $L$  are  $\mathit{\infty}$ *-isogonal* if the the projections of  $K$ ,  $L$  onto  $BC$  are equidistant from the midpoint of  $BC$ ,  $M$  (we say this because if  $T_{\infty}$  is the point at infinity along the perpendicular to  $BC$ , then  $T_{\infty}K$  and  $T_{\infty}L$  are isogonal with respect to angle  $\angle BT_{\infty} C$ )**Claim.** Points  $I_O$  and and  $I_U$  are  $\infty$ -isogonal.
