**Solution:** Let the incircle be  $\omega$  and the circumcircle be  $\Omega$  and their radical axis be  $l$ . The main idea is to apply Monge's Theorem to the circles  $\omega$ ,  $\omega_1$ , and  $\omega_2$ . We will show that the exmilicenter of  $\omega$  and  $\omega_1$  lies on  $l$  and similarly that the exmilicenter of  $\omega$  and  $\omega_2$  lies on  $l$ , implying the result.

Let  $I$  be the center of  $\omega$  and  $J$  be the center of  $\omega_1$ . Let  $X=IJ\cap AC$  be the exmilicenter of  $\omega$  and  $\omega_1$ .  Let  $\gamma =(AIC)$ . By the Shooting Lemma, inversion about  $\gamma$  preserves  $w_1$ . By the Orthogonality Lemma, inversion about  $w_1$  preserves  $\gamma$ . Let  $I$  and  $E$  be mapped to  $I'$  and  $E'$  under this inversion about  $w_1$ . Clearly  $I'\in \gamma$ . Since  $E'$  is the midpoint of the chord of the polar of  $E$ ,  Saywayama's Theorem implies that  $\angle IE'J=90^{\circ}$  and thus by inversion,  $\angle EI'J=90^{\circ}$ . Finally, we have  $X\in l$  because   $$ \mathcal{P}_{\omega}(X)=XE^2=XI'\cdot XI=\mathcal{P}_{\gamma}(X)=XA\cdot XC=\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(X). $$  [asy]
